\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ/ \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 /Nikkei -2: Cites Unnamed Market Sources \b0\par\par\uc2 291 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 1999\par 06:05\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Nikkei)--The Bank of Japan stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Tuesday morning to contain the yen's further rise against the U.S. dollar, the Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported in its Tuesday evening edition, citing market sources. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is estimated to have bought $1 billion-$2 billion for yen in the first such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since February 1996. \par \par \uc2  The yen's climb to a 29-month high has fueled concern the stronger yen will exacerbate Japan's economic slump by intensifying deflationary pressure and cutting into exports. Following the BOJ action, the dollar reversed direction, rising above Y111.50 at one point. \par \par \uc2  The bank appears to have unilaterally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to sell the yen intermittently through Japanese and foreign banks since around 10 a.m. local time (0100 GMT or 8 p.m. EST Monday). \par \par \uc2  The bank aims to show the market it will not to tolerate the yen's strengthening beyond Y110, analysts said. Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa told reporters the same day he is "keeping a close eye on recent yen movements." \par \par \uc2  The yen edged higher to the dollar since late last year on concerns over the slowdown of the U.S. economy and a possible resurgence of turmoil in Latin American markets. Business leaders have voiced anxiety about the adverse effect of the strong yen on earnings. \par \par \uc2  The government and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because they fear the yen's steep ascent, coupled with lower bond and stock prices, may depress the economy further, the analysts noted. \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded at Y111.35-Y111.38 at noon, up Y1.15 from Monday's close. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 12-01-99 \par \par \uc2  0505GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv1c007mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
