\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen beaten back, overshadowed by BOJ. \b0\par\par\uc2 818 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 1999\par 10:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 12 (Reuters) - The dollar and euro won back some of their recent large losses against the yen on Tuesday, with apparent dollar/yen buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spurring widespread selling of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  While Tokyo authorities did not confirm they had been in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to push down the yen, which on Monday rose to its strongest in 28 months against the dollar, traders in London said the price action strongly indicated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In early European trade, Tokyo bankers said the Bank of Japan was seen buying dollars intermittently from around 111 yen. Earlier in the Asian session, traders said the BOJ had likely started buying dollars at below 110 yen. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like the BOJ has started to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in earnest," said one trader at a Japanese bank in London. "After dollar/yen bounced up on the initial \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  it was starting to stall at around 111 (yen). They were probably afraid it would be driven back down again and so gave it another push." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 112.55/65 yen at 0838 GMT, off its overnight high of 112.80 which was a rebound of more than four percent from the low of 108.17 yen reached on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Moves in euro/yen tracked those of dollar/yen. Europe's new single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was last at 129.20/40 yen, up nearly four percent from the low of 124.55 seen on Monday which was its weakest rate since its market debut on January 4. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top officials stepped up their rhetoric on the yen but steered clear of commenting on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said recent moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market were "a bit rapid", but said that while he was carefully monitoring the movements, he had not received any report on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's top government spokesman Hiromu Nonaka was quoted as saying by Japanese news agency Jiji that an excessive rise in the yen was not desirable for the world economy. \par \par \uc2  While the apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the rhetoric helped fend off dollar/yen bears, traders and analysts were not convinced that sentiment in the pair had changed substantially. \par \par \uc2  They said there was still substantial demand for the yen in the market due to investor interest in the Japanese government bond market, where yields have risen rapidly over the past month or so. \par \par \uc2  "It's still not obvious whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be sufficient to change the yen's trend right now," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  "Japan's long-term interest rates are rising. While the Japanese authorities have now signalled they are unhappy with dollar/yen below 110, it's unlikely it will rebound very strongly," she said. \par \par \uc2  The market's attention would now be riveted to the United States, with dollar/yen's fate expected to hinge on what Washington says, or does not say, about recent developments. \par \par \uc2  Late on Monday, a spokesman at the U.S. Treasury Department a published report saying it was little concerned with the dollar's slide did not reflect the Treasury's views. \par \par \uc2  "I remain a little sceptical whether it (dollar/yen's gain) will hold," said Jeff Woodruff, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of Boston. "It depends what happens in New York. If the Federal Reserve says it supports this move, then maybe we could get up to 115." \par \par \uc2  Apart from yen-related issues, traders cited dollar-bearish factors which they said was likely to prevent a large rebound in dollar/yen. \par \par \uc2  Concern about the U.S. economy was a major factor, now that worries about Brazil have come back to the fore with last week's declaration by a Brazilian state to suspend its debt payments to the central government. \par \par \uc2  This in turn would keep the market focused on the U.S. stock market, with fears that further signs of crisis in Latin America could spark a selloff in the Dow, which rose to a record high earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the euro continued to lose ground against the dollar to hit its lowest rate since its birth earlier on Tuesday. Euro/dollar was last at $1.1480/84, up from its trough of $1.1440 which was a drop of four percent from the peak seen on January 4. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market was returning to look at the economic fundamentals of the United States and Europe, with the former appearing to gain the upper hand. \par \par \uc2  "There was an initial euro rally to celebrate the launch. But it wasn't really backed by changes in fundamentals and in that case, whatever was bought up is going to be sold off," said a trader at a Japanese bank in London. \par \par \uc2  ((Chizu Nomiyama, London newsroom 44 171 542 6721, chizu.nomiyama@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1c06nru\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
