\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo Stocks End Down;  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Jitters Despite Yen Retreat \b0\par\par\uc2 301 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 1999\par 07:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks closed slightly lower Tuesday after gyrating between positive and negative territory on the wild fluctuation of the dollar-yen rate. \par \par \uc2  The market opened down after the dollar fell to Y108.21 for the first time since August 1996. But shares rebounded sharply on the dollar's subsequent rally as high as Y111.80 after talk the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Public funds were seen buying Nikkei futures to support the market, but such upward pressure was limited," said Kunihiro Hatae, general manager at Tokyo Securities Co. "Depending on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, the stock market may try more on the downside," \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei average of 225 selected issues fell 7.51 points to 13,360.97, after falling as low as 13,212.30 and rising as high as 13,535.66. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also rallied strongly in the morning after Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said he is closely monitoring the recent rise in the yen. \par \par \uc2  The Nihon Keizai Shimbun also reported the Bank of Japan sold yen for dollars Tuesday morning for the first time since February 1996, spending $1 billion to $2 billion, though some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders were skeptical that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Some exporter shares trimmed losses as investors welcomed the dollar's rise. A stronger yen hurts exporters' price competitiveness and reduces their overseas earnings when repatriated into yen. \par \par \uc2  Bridgestone, Tokyo Electron, NEC, and Olympus ended moderately higher. But Toyota, Fuji Photo, Kyocera, Advantest, Fujitsu, Canon and Hoya were weaker. \par \par \uc2  Sony lost Y70 to Y7,390, marking the ninth straight session of declines. TDK was down Y90 at Y9,210 for the fourth day of losses. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  12-01-99 \par \par \uc2  0640GMT \par \par \uc2  4159 5011 55117 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020010824dv1c005m1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
