\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Yen jumps, bulls ready to run at sign of BOJ. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chizu Nomiyama \par \uc2 729 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 1999\par 13:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 11 (Reuters) - The yen stampeded to its highest in more than two years against the dollar on Monday, but traders grew wary of provoking the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  into pushing the yen down. \par \par \uc2  The yen's gain of two percent on Monday to a 28-month high was the latest leg of a rally that began at the beginning of December. \par \par \uc2  Investor eagerness to buy Japanese government bonds, whose yields have doubled in the past month, fuelled flows into the yen. On the other side of the equation, worries about Brazil's economy put pressure on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "We are now at a level where the Bank of Japan would be justified in expressing its displeasure at developments, either through direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or by dollar buying from semi-governmental institutions," said Rob Miniken, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slid to 108.58 yen, a fall of almost five percent from a week ago. \par \par \uc2  The mood among yen bulls alternated between confidence and nervousness as they drove dollar/yen lower and then shied away as they envisaged a backlash of yen sales from the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  "People are now starting to get the feeling that the fall in dollar/yen is a bit overdone," said one trader at a British bank. "They're keeping an eye out for the BOJ, of course, although they're not all that worried. If it did come in, it would just give the market something to sell into." \par \par \uc2  The market was now eyeing the 105-yen level as the next goal with some analysts citing the key 100 figure. \par \par \uc2  Top-level Japanese officials have started to send warning signals about the recent gyrations in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. On Monday, vice finance minister Koji Tanami said Tokyo would take "appropriate measures" in the event of drastic fluctuations and said excessive moves would be unfavourable. Still, many analysts said it was unlikely the Bank of Japan would jump in to sell yen now. \par \par \uc2  "Obviously the BOJ is going to be concerned and there is a possibility of their coming in at 105, and that level will probably be the market's target in the short term," said James Mitchell, senior strategist at Nomura International. \par \par \uc2  "We've had several Japanese officials mentioning a range of 110 to 120 recently. It's hard to pinpoint an exact target like that in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, and a few yen should not be much of a concern for the authorities." \par \par \uc2  While the yen has shot higher in recent days, analysts noted it was still near the middle of the range it has seen over the past four years. In April 1995, dollar/yen went as low as 79.70, while in August last year it reached a high of 147.63. \par \par \uc2  Nor is dollar/yen moving at the breakneck pace it showed in October, when the dollar fell a stunning 17 percent in three days. Traders still look back on those few days with a shiver, but there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  then. \par \par \uc2  "They (the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) have never had a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  target. They've let dollar/yen go down to 80 in the past so you can forget about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this stage," said Peter von Maydell, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston. \par \par \uc2  Such a slide in dollar/yen seems puzzling because Japan's economic situation remains dire. The economy has been in recession for four straight quarters and is suffering from an ailing financial industry. Analysts say there that dollar weakness is part of the equation. \par \par \uc2  "It's still pretty much a strong yen story, though it's also becoming more of a weak dollar story with the worries about Brazil and the impeachment trial (of U.S. President Clinton)," said Mitchell at Nomura. "The impeachment is not going to have much of an effect, but given the direction of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , people are using it as an excuse to drive it down further." \par \par \uc2  Concerns about the economy of Latin America - a crucial trading partner for the United States - came to the fore late last week after a Brazilian state declared a moratorium on its debt payments to the central government. \par \par \uc2  ((London newsroom 44 171 542 6721, chizu.nomiyama@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1b06tpk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
