\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Yen bludgeons dollar, squashes euro. \b0\par\par\uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 1999\par 13:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 7 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar crashed briefly below 110 yen on Thursday for the first time since September 1996 and the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also squashed down the euro, Europe's infant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still in its first week of trading. \par \par \uc2  The yen rallied as far as 109.82 per dollar, a 27-month high, its strength all the greater because of concern the dollar could suffer from renewed Brazilian debt worries and U.S. President Bill Clinton's impeachment trial. \par \par \uc2  The dollar quickly spiked back up to above 111 yen, amid talk of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but traders and analysts dismissed the talk and doubted the fall had been arrested. \par \par \uc2  "I think we are going to head towards 105," said Kirit Shah, chief strategist at Sanwa International in London. \par \par \uc2  Like many market players he expected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent losses below 105 yen. Japanese officials, worried about an overly strong yen, restricted themselves to verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with Vice Finance Minister Koji Tanami saying in Tokyo that "rapid fluctuations must be avoided." \par \par \uc2  The story was a strong yen across the board. The euro was driven down overnight to 128 yen, its lowest level since the start of trading in the new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on January 4. \par \par \uc2  By 1115 GMT the euro was at 129.55/75 yen, still giving plenty of food for thought for the 17 members of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governing council who began their first post-launch meeting in Frankfurt on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the yen's surge was largely due to domestic factors with Japanese investors attracted by their own government's bonds (JGBs). There was strong demand for Thursday's auction of 10-year bonds worth 1.8 trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar had other problems. Thursday's scheduled start of what could be a protracted impeachment trial for Clinton was seen as dollar-negative by traders. \par \par \uc2  So was Wednesday's announcement by the Brazilian state of Minas Gerais of a 90-day moratorium on debt payments to central government - triggering fears of a renewed threat to the dollar and the Dow because of high U.S. exposure to Brazil. \par \par \uc2  "Brazil is obviously a wild card for the United States market," said Sanwa's Shah. \par \par \uc2  Ironically, the yen's big moves came as Japanese Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi began a seven-day visit to the three core euro countries to press the case for stable \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and for the yen's continued presence in the super-league of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the dollar and the euro. \par \par \uc2  Obuchi was seeing French President Jacques Chirac in Paris on Thursday - before heading to Italy and Germany - and the two leaders were due to make a joint statement on the international financial system, a senior Japanese official said. \par \par \uc2  In Brussels, German Finance Minister Oskar Lafontaine said he wanted the debate on creating a new set of checks on international financial markets to continue. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England defied the predictions of most economists on Thursday when its monetary policy committee (MPC) cut the key repo rate for the fourth month running. \par \par \uc2  The MPC trimmed the rate by 25 basis points to six percent - still twice the level in the three core European economies - and said British economic data and survey evidence continued to point to a slowing economy. \par \par \uc2  Sterling dropped to $1.6485, a session low, after the news but was steady against the euro at 70.63 pence. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was above its 17-1/2 month lows against the rampaging yen, trading at 183.82/92 by 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  ((Nicholas Kotch,London Newsroom, +44 171 542 7718, nick.kotch@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv17072gq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
