\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Yen muscles higher, euro loses ground. \b0\par\par\uc2 761 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 1999\par 11:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 7 (Reuters) - The yen vaulted higher against the dollar on Thursday as the market made a dash for the magnetic 110 yen to the dollar level, while the euro slipped against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and softened against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Traders noted that dollar/yen's spike down to 110 yen, its lowest in 27 months, in early European trade took place amid thin volume. \par \par \uc2  While dollar sentiment was soft ahead of the U.S. Senate's impeachment trial on President Bill Clinton, traders and analysts said Thursday's move in dollar/yen had more to do with market dynamics than the economic fundamentals of the United States or Japan. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 110.64/69 yen at 0900 GMT after dropping to 110.07 earlier, compared with 112.92 seen in late Wednesday's trade. Euro/yen was quoted at 129.22/40 yen after touching 128.00, which was its weakest rate since it first started trading on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The lows mean euro/yen has shed 5.5 percent since the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's birth, while dollar/yen has weakened by four percent since the start of the week. "It doesn't look like there's much capital flow going through there. The market's been fixated on this 110 yen level (in dollar/yen) which has been like a magnet," said Ian Gunner, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in London. \par \par \uc2  "It's going to be a tough area to break but if it does, we could see 106," Gunner added. \par \par \uc2  Traders said dollar/yen's choppiness around the 110-yen level on Thursday was due to selling by participants who wanted to trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options with strikes at that level, and buying by those who wanted to prevent this. \par \par \uc2  "There isn't all that much volume going through, but it's going to be a big struggle today between the options-related buying and selling," said one trader at a Japanese bank in London. "It looks like the market doesn't want to give up until it sees whether it can get through 110." \par \par \uc2  In terms of fundamentals, the dollar was being dampened also by worries about Latin America's economic situation after the Brazilian state of Minas Gerais announced on Wednesday it was suspending payments of its debt to the central government. But on the positive side, the Brazilian senate approved a key part of the government's fiscal savings plan. \par \par \uc2  Another dollar-bearish factor was concern about a heightening of tension between the United States and Japan over the latter's huge trade surplus. Japan's vice trade minister Osamu Watanabe said on Thursday that he expects U.S. criticism of Japan over trade issues to increase. \par \par \uc2  With dollar/yen struggling near its lowest in 27 months the market is on alert for any signs that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, although traders and analysts said this was unlikely until dollar/yen dropped decisively below 110. \par \par \uc2  "There is talk the BOJ might come in to stop the yen strengthening further, but there won't be any action until it breaks below 110. The first line of action will be jawboning," said Ian Morris, international economist at HSBC Securities. \par \par \uc2  Gunner at ABN Amro said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be loath to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless it was reasonably sure of success, and that for that reason it might wait until dollar/yen had dropped further and the short yen positions cleared out of the market. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro had softened against the dollar but traders and analysts were unperturbed, noting that it has stuck to a range of 2.5 percent since its debut on Monday and saying that it was still quite stable. \par \par \uc2  There was little reason for the market to get too bullish or bearish on euro/dollar yet as there had not been any changes in economic fundamentals since the euro was born, they added. \par \par \uc2  "It's early days yet and volume isn't as high as it was (for dollar/mark). There's bound to be a bit of choppiness in the beginning...and you can't read too much into it," said Morris. \par \par \uc2  The euro was last quoted at $1.1665/70 compared to its session low of $1.1616, which was its lowest rate since it started trading on Monday. Euro/dollar was at $1.1647 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  ((Chizu Nomiyama, London newsroom 44 171 542 6721, chizu.nomiyama@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv17071ea\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
