\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo dealers eye  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  near 100-105 yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tamawa Kadoya \par \uc2 546 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 1999\par 11:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 6 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may conduct dollar-buying, yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should the dollar fall to a 100 to 105 yen range, as the risks of a "freefall" would emerge if the dollar broke 110 yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts and dealers said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "There is a chance that the dollar may freefall if it breaks below 110 yen," said Taisuke Tanaka, director and strategist for global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Credit Suisse First Boston in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "That would make the 100 to 105 yen range a defensive zone... and may prompt Japanese authorities to step in and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Tanaka said. \par \par \uc2  Yasuharu Tsuru, manager of the international treasury division at Mitsubishi Trust and Banking, however, told Reuters Television: "A range of 105 to 110 yen range may be acceptable for now." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the dollar's fall may sharply accelerate below 110 yen, leading Japanese investors to rush to hedge their dollar-denominated holdings and financial institutions to spur repatriation of funds ahead of the annual book-closing at the end of March. \par \par \uc2  As such, "a higher yen would feed a further rise in the yen," Tanaka said. \par \par \uc2  Many dealers had expected Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise warning flags before the dollar approached 110 yen after Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said in early December that the United States was "comfortable" with the dollar in a 110 to 120 yen range. \par \par \uc2  As the dollar tumbled toward the low end of that range on Tuesday, Miyazawa declined to comment on specific dollar/yen exchange levels and said he would like to monitor developments in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Top financial diplomat Eisuke Sakakibara remained equally mum about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels, but his "no comment" remarks fed speculation that the Finance Ministry was giving tacit approval for current levels. \par \par \uc2  "It is unclear what the MOF is intending, but the dollar has remained within the 110 to 120 yen range, so they may be taking a wait-and-see attitude," Tanaka said. \par \par \uc2  Monetary authorities would likely jawbone the markets if the dollar breaks below 110 yen, but Mitsubishi Trust's Tsuru doubted whether they would actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar at that level. \par \par \uc2  Some traders remained sceptical about whether monetary authorities would really step in to stem the dollar's fall. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fever is not gripping the markets," said a senior dealer at U.S. bank, who declined to be named. \par \par \uc2  If monetary authorities refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar could fall below 100 yen, they said. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, the United States likely welcomed a gradual decline in the dollar as long as it did not spur capital outflows from dollar assets, an economist at a major city bank said. \par \par \uc2  Speculation on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top government spokesman Hiromu Nonaka said on Tuesday that Japan will step up its monitoring of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and take "appropriate" action in cooperation with other countries. \par \par \uc2  Belatedly, it had an effect. Traders said the dollar's fall was halted after touching 110.40 yen overnight - a level not seen since September 1996 - partly by vague talk of the Bank of Japan checking rates. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8027 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv16076ry\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
