\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Debutante euro upstaged by dowager yen's rally. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chizu Nomiyama \par \uc2 733 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 1999\par 14:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 5 (Reuters) - The euro took a back seat to the yen on Tuesday as the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  staged a stunning rally against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The yen shot up by more than three percent from levels seen on Monday to its highest in 27 months against the dollar, the trigger being some yen-bullish comments from Eisuke Sakakibara, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs. \par \par \uc2  But analysts were quick to point out the yen's latest rally was based more on market supply-and-demand conditions, namely Japanese investors' seasonal need for their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than any cheerful news about the nation's economy. \par \par \uc2  "Is the yen rally justified? In terms of economic fundamentals, no," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi in London. "But until the end of the Japanese fiscal year (on March 31), it's not going to trade on fundamentals, it will be supply and demand factors." \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to 110.46 yen in European trade on Tuesday, its lowest since September 1996, though it had recovered to above 111 by the midsession. \par \par \uc2  The yen jumped after Sakakibara - known as Mr Yen because of the influence his views have on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market - said the U.S. economy had entered a slowdown. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also said the yen would rise should Japan's economy pick up this year. \par \par \uc2  Until a couple of months ago, yen bullishness seemed almost a contradiction in terms, considering the state of the Japanese economy, which has been in recession for four straight quarters. \par \par \uc2  But a flagging dollar, worn down by the market's worries about the impact of the Asian and Latin American crises on the U.S. economy, has given the yen a lift since then. \par \par \uc2  The yen has rallied 25 percent against the dollar since touching an eight-year low 147.63 in August last year. \par \par \uc2  Now there is an additional boost, as many Japanese companies repatriate assets denominated in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for their balance sheets ahead of the financial year-end. \par \par \uc2  A recent slide in the Japanese government bond (JGB) market and the ensuing rise in bond yields has also helped the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The yen is now an attractive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the dollar under pressure all around. That's the main feature at the moment," said Tim Fox, chief economist at Standard Chartered here. \par \par \uc2  "In terms of (Japanese economic) growth, I don't think this yen strength is sustainable. But the yen's move is reflecting Japanese interest rates. JGB yields look like they're going to stay in place." \par \par \uc2  But analysts note that while the yield of the key 10-year JGB may have doubled over the past month, that is not as stunning as it may seem, given that the bond now yields just under two percent, compared with around one percent in early December. It is still far below 4.7 percent for U.S. Treasuries. \par \par \uc2  While the factors may be temporary, traders and analysts say the dollar/yen pair is in a soft phase for now, adding that at current levels the key numbers 110 and 100 would be too tempting for dollar bears. \par \par \uc2  "I've been bearish on dollar/yen for quite a while, targetting 105...and it looks like we're going to see that over the next few weeks very easily," said Kirit Shah, chief strategist at Sanwa International in London. \par \par \uc2  This, in turn, would raise the spectre of dollar/yen buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders are already on red alert for any signs that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in the market, and such rumours helped the dollar bounce off its lows on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But they said the BOJ would likely hold its fire and step in only when the pair looked like it was speeding towards 100. \par \par \uc2  "If it drops below 110 there is certainly a risk the BOJ will sit up and take notice....but it would be a bit premature to assume the BOJ will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as soon as it gets below 110," said Halpenny at BOT-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ is well aware that the market is moving on supply and demand rather than fundamentals now. The fear is a drop below 100," he added. \par \par \uc2  ((London newsroom 44 171 542 6721, chizu.nomiyama@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv150751d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
