\par\par \b \uc2  Coordinated G7 move to prop yen unlikely - analysts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Astrid Zweynert \par \uc2 690 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 1998\par 14:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 29 (Reuters) - The weakness of the yen will be a prime concern for officials from the world's richest nations when they meet this weekend but analysts said on Tuesday they were sceptical of a coordinated plan to stem yen declines. \par \par \uc2  Speculation about a policy agreement by the Group of Seven nations (G7) mounted after Kyodo news agency quoted an unnamed international monetary source on Monday as saying G7 officials were close to agreeing on policy coordination. \par \par \uc2  G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors meet in Washington on Saturday. Items expected to be on the agenda include the recent turmoil in global financial markets. \par \par \uc2  "There are a lot of international issues to be discussed at the meeting, and it still looks like the dollar/yen exchange rate is a bilateral issue between the U.S. and Japan," said David Coleman, chief economist at CIBC World Markets. "The G7 as a whole can't really do a lot about the yen's weakness, so it's likely they will confine themselves to words, not action." \par \par \uc2  The Kyodo report coincided with comments about the yen by U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin on Monday. Rubin expressed concern about the weak yen and said his concern was shared by Japan. He also reiterated that the U.S. dollar policy - a policy which favours a strong dollar - remained unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Rubin's comments and the Kyodo report pushed the yen up to a high of 133.72 per dollar in European trading, up from 135.50 in late trade on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said U.S. monetary authorities are likely to be concerned that investors, faced with crises in Asia, Russia and Latin America, will increasingly withdraw funds from emerging markets, and a stronger dollar may encourage such withdrawal of capital. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) was reported in mid-August - after the yen had fallen to an 8-1/2 year low near 148 per dollar - as considering a rate of about 130 yen as appropriate. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S and the G7 want to ensure yen stability and prevent a fresh bout of weakness," said Tim Fox, chief economist at Standard Chartered. "There is likely to be concern that flows out of Japan will accelerate again when the fiscal half-year is over at the end of this month." \par \par \uc2  Moreover, the Bank of Japan's quarterly survey of business sentiment for the July-September period, due at 2350 GMT on Wednesday, is likely to spell bad news for the yen as the world's second-biggest economy remains mired in recession. \par \par \uc2  "The Tankan will be extremely downbeat in its views and so further highlight what a mess the Japanese economy is in," said David Brickman, international economist at Paine Webber. \par \par \uc2  The yen's weakness is less dominant now than throughout this summer when it hit eight-year lows against the dollar and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly in its support. \par \par \uc2  But a fresh decline of the yen would present international monetary authorities with a key concern because it would add to devaluation pressure on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the Chinese yuan, and could throw global financial markets into further turmoil. \par \par \uc2  A senior Chinese official said on Tuesday China faced no pressure to devalue the yuan. But newspapers reported the People's Bank of China had ordered Chinese firms to repatriate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  held overseas to combat speculators betting against the yuan. \par \par \uc2  A yuan devaluation is seen as likely to worsen the Asian crisis and spark off competitive devaluations across the region. \par \par \uc2  Even though the weak yen gives a boon to Japanese exporters, Japan has reason to oppose a softer yen because it can reduce the attractiveness of Japanese equities and bonds. \par \par \uc2  A widely expected U.S. interest rate cut at the Federal Open Market Committee meeting on Tuesday would contribute to stabilising the yen as lower U.S. rates reduce the appeal of dollar-denominated deposits, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  ((London newsroom + 44 171 542 7792 fax +44 171 542 5293, astrid.zweynert@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du9t00xrb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
