\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen up, market gripped by FOMC fever. \b0\par\par\uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 1998\par 16:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 29 (Reuters) - Talk of a co-ordinated international move to stem the yen's weakness buoyed the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday at the expense of the dollar and the mark. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market's attention was firmly focused on the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) meeting on Tuesday amid expectations that FOMC will opt for a cut in U.S. interest rates for the first time in 32 months. \par \par \uc2  Speculation about a policy agreement by the Group of Seven nations (G7) mounted after Kyodo news agency quoted an unnamed international monetary source on Monday as saying G7 officials were close to agreeing on policy coordination. \par \par \uc2  G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors meet in Washington on Saturday. Items expected to be on the agenda include the recent turmoil in global financial markets. \par \par \uc2  The report coincided with comments about the yen by U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin on Monday. Rubin expressed concern about the weak yen and said his concern was shared by Japan. He also reiterated that the U.S.'s dollar policy - which favours a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - remained unchanged. \par \par \uc2  "The FOMC meeting is the key thing markets are watching but the yen has strengthened on short-covering on the Rubin comments and the Kyodo report," said a senior trader at a German bank. \par \par \uc2  The yen rose to a high of 133.50 in European afternoon trade, up from 135.58/63 in late trade on Monday. It also rose to 79.58 per mark before shedding a little ground to trade at 79.76/80 at 1415 GMT, still up from 81.14/16 late on Monday. Dollar/mark was little changed at 1.6766/71. \par \par \uc2  Analysts were sceptical about a joint policy move by the G7 nations and said the yen's rise looked short-lived. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S and the G7 want to ensure yen stability and prevent a fresh bout of weakness," said Tim Fox, chief economist at Standard Chartered. "There is...concern that flows out of Japan will accelerate again when the fiscal half-year is over at the end of this month." \par \par \uc2  Moreover, the Bank of Japan's quarterly survey of business sentiment for the July-September period, due at 2350 GMT on Wednesday, is likely to spell bad news for the yen as the world's second-biggest economy remains mired in recession. \par \par \uc2  "The Tankan will be extremely downbeat in its views and so further highlight what a mess the Japanese economy is in," said David Brickman, international economist at Paine Webber. \par \par \uc2  The yen's weakness is less dominant now than throughout this summer when it hit eight-year lows against the dollar and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly in its support. \par \par \uc2  But a fresh decline of the yen would present international monetary authorities with a key concern because it would add to devaluation pressure on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the Chinese yuan, and could throw financial markets into further turmoil. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the FOMC to opt for a rate cut in a bid to shield the U.S. economy from the threat of slowing growth due to crises in emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  A 25 basis points easing is unlikely to move the dollar much as such a cut has been discounted, dealers said. A more agggressive cut could even buoy the dollar because of an expected positive reaction on Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  The mark was feeling mild after-effects of last Sunday's German election and the end of 16 years of centre-right rule in Europe's largest economy. Assumptions that Gerhard Schroeder's Social Democrats will form a coalition with the leftist Green Party are seen as a mark negative in the short-term. \par \par \uc2  SPD Chairman Oscar Lafontaine, favourite to become finance minister in the new government, said on Monday interest rate cuts in Europe would be the right way to fight unemployment. \par \par \uc2  ((London newsroom + 44 171 542 7792 fax +44 171 542 5293,astrid.zweynert@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du9t00xm7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
