\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-BOJ credit easing fails to inspire Tokyo markets. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Keiko Takagi \par \uc2 559 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1998\par 09:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 10 (Reuters) - A surprise credit easing by the Bank of Japan (BOJ) failed to inspire confidence in the nation's troubled economy, with Tokyo stocks erasing earlier marginal gains to close lower on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The credit easing, while sending yen bond yields to record lows, capped the dollar's topside against the yen by spurring speculation that the U.S. Federal Reserve may follow suit. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei average closed down 89.51 points or 0.61 percent at 14,666.03. September futures fell 110 points to 14,600. By comparison, bonds rose, pushing yields to historic lows. \par \par \uc2  "It makes an extreme situation more extreme," said Michael Wilkins, a dealer at Credit Lyonnais. "At the end of the day, it's hard to see a net positive effect on the real economy." \par \par \uc2  The BOJ - in its first monetary policy change in more than three years - said late on Wednesday it would guide the overnight call rate on average to around 0.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  The rate had traded at just below the 0.5 percent discount rate for much of the past three years. \par \par \uc2  "I'm sceptical about how much the BOJ's move will help boost the ailing domestic economy, although it will help put a brake on deflationary pressures at home," said Kenji Karikomi, deputy general manager of Daiwa Securities. \par \par \uc2  Banks and brokerages, among the sectors most sensitive to changes in interest rates, were sold off in late trading. The banking subindex was off 0.7 percent, while securities slumped 4.72 percent. But Fuji Bank, hard hit on Wednesday, was up four yen at 333. \par \par \uc2  In New York overnight, the Dow fell as a string of profit warnings and concerns about the stability of President Bill Clinton's administration roiled investor sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Against an increasingly grim domestic and international backdrop, traders said investors will be quick to take profits if the Nikkei popped above 15,000. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ's decision will be neutral for share prices," said Yoshikazu Kanbayashi, head of the second equity trading division at Kankaku Securities. "It does nothing to change the outlook for gloomy corporate earnings." \par \par \uc2  The dollar meanwhile hovered at around 135.70 yen in late Tokyo trading, down from its New York close of about 136.70. \par \par \uc2  "Speculation that the Fed could follow the BOJ's move is likely to prevent the dollar from reaching 140 yen in the near term," said a dealer at a Japanese city bank. \par \par \uc2  Also capping the dollar's topside was an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  warning from a Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Eisuke Sakakibara, vice finance minister for international affairs, told Reuters on Thursday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market whenever necessary. He also said the ministry welcomed the BOJ's decision to ease credit. \par \par \uc2  The yield of the benchmark 182nd 10-year JGB fell to a global record low of 0.840 percent in late afternoon, compared with the previous low of 0.995 percent on September 1. \par \par \uc2  "Investors' buying of JGBs was stronger than many had expected," said a senior dealer at a city bank's securities unit. "Many gave up waiting for a bargain-hunting chance and started to buy near highs." ((Tokyo Equities Desk +813 3432-9404 tokyo.equities.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9a00rtm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\png