\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar slides vs yen, mark in early Tokyo. \b0\par\par\uc2 435 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1998\par 02:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      TOKYO, Sept 10 (Reuters) - ASIAN \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ACTIVITY: \par \par \uc2      0022 GMT -- \par \par \uc2      *Dollar slid against yen and mark in early Tokyo on \par \par \uc2  Thursday as Japanese investors locked in profits ahead of \par \par \uc2  September book-closing. *Dollar also pressured by U.S. fund \par \par \uc2  operators unwinding dollar-long positions. \par \par \uc2  *Some U.S. operators picking up dollar on dips. Expected \par \par \uc2  commercial demand at 0100 GMT retailing fixing to support \par \par \uc2  greenback. \par \par \uc2  *Dollar quoted at 135.05/10 yen and 1.7086/96 marks against \par \par \uc2  136.65/75 yen and 1.7190/00 marks in late New York Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  *Dollar briefly dipped against yen as comments by Finance Ministry officials kept \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears afloat. \par \par \uc2  *Senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Haruhiko Kuroda said on Thursday \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at an appropriate time if necessary. \par \par \uc2  *Kuroda also said a correction of yen's weakness has begun. \par \par \uc2  *Earlier, Eisuke Sakakibara, vice finance minister for international affairs, told Reuters that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market whenever necessary. \par \par \uc2  *Tokyo stocks fell into negative territory after opening firmer, supported by BOJ's decision to ease credit on Wednesday. Key Nikkei average down 42.75 points at 14,712.79 as of 0037 GMT. \par \par \uc2  *Consensus building that dollar to fall within 133 to 138 yen range due to speculation of U.S. rate cut until U.S. Federal Open Market Committee meeting on September 29. \par \par \uc2  2308 GMT - \par \par \uc2  *Dollar topside heavy against yen in early Asia on Thursday on strong selling pressure from Japanese investors and overseas funds. \par \par \uc2  *Dollar unlikely to head higher despite Bank of Japan's decision on Wednesday to guide key short-term interest rate lower. Dollar expected to meet resistance near 137 yen after reaching high of 138.30 yen overnight. \par \par \uc2  *"No one expects (the BOJ's easing action) to prompt a dollar rise towards 140 yen," a dealer at a trust bank said. \par \par \uc2  *Greenback seen supported by commercial demand at retail fixing. \par \par \uc2  *The BOJ decided on Wednesday to guide the overnight call rate to a record low 0.25 percent from around 0.45 percent. \par \par \uc2  *BOJ also said it will supply as much liquidity to money market as needed regardless of overnight call rate target. \par \par \uc2  *Speculation of possible coordinated easing by U.S. Federal Reserve to cap dollar rise. \par \par \uc2  *Dollar fell against yen and mark overnight in New York as investors took profits in stock market and worried about Russia's financial crisis, as well as U.S. President Bill Clinton's political fate. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8027 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9a00req\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
