\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo Stocks Erase Early Gains, Close 0.6% Lower Despite Rate Cut \b0\par\par\uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1998\par 08:14\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Tokyo stocks closed lower Thursday after erasing early gains despite the Bank of Japan's decision late Wednesday to ease monetary policy for the first time in three years. \par \par \uc2  In Japan, the Nikkei 225 index fell 89.51 points, or 0.6%, to close at 14666.03, extending Wednesday's 157.95-point loss. Losers edged winners 2 to 1 as 364 million shares changed hands. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip index climbed in the first moments of trading, only to quickly turn lower. By midmorning, stocks had clawed their way back into positive territory and were up 73.83 points, or 0.5%, at midday. \par \par \uc2  Investor sentiment was boosted by a decision late Wednesday by the Bank of Japan to ease its monetary stance. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to guide overnight call money rates - the rates banks charge each other to borrow money overnight without collateral - lower to around 0.25% from the previous 0.50%, a measure taken to prevent Japan's economy from falling into a deflationary spiral. But market participants said the rate cut wasn't nearly enough to offset worries about Japan's rickety banking system and global-financial turmoil. Traders also are closely watching ongoing deliberations in Parliament over bills to revive Japan's financial industry. \par \par \uc2  "Getting action on banking reform is much more important than a small rate cut," said Keiko Kondo, a strategist at Merrill Lynch. The ruling Liberal Democratic Party and the opposition remain deadlocked over legislation to restructure the banking system. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, investor speculation that the U.S. may cut interest rates, as well as political uncertainty about President Clinton encouraged dollar selling, traders said. Independent Prosecutor Kenneth Starr delivered to Congress a report on his investigation into Clinton's admitted affair with a former White House intern. \par \par \uc2  In late Asian trading, the dollar was quoted at 135.50 yen, down sharply from 136.85 yen late Wednesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  "There are a lot of factors weighing on the dollar," a Japanese trader said. "The Clinton scandal appears to be getting more serious ... But another large factor is the possibility that the (U.S.) Federal Reserve may cut interest rates." \par \par \uc2  Lower interest rates erode returns for investors and make the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  less attractive. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Haruhiko Kuroda, the head of the international bureau at \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry, said that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the yen if it falls too low. \par \par \uc2  Worries about global turmoil returned to haunt the securities sector after Daiwa Securities said Wednesday it had unrealized trading losses of about 14 billion yen on its emerging-market bond holdings, due partly to the Russian economic crisis. The sector tumbled 4.7%, making it the biggest decliner of the day. Daiwa Securities sank 37 yen to 410 yen, Nikko Securities plunged 26 yen to 388 yen and Nomura dropped 27 yen to 1,275 yen. \par \par \uc2  Banks fell 0.7% amid worries about the banking-bill debate and unwinding of cross-shareholdings ahead of the Sept. 30 end of the fiscal half-year. Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi fell 7 yen to 1,067 yen, Mitsui Trust & Banking lost 7 yen to 168 yen and Sanwa Bank declined 11 yen to 909 yen. \par \par \uc2  Fuji Bank stabilized, gaining 4 yen to 333 yen after it tumbled 59 yen Wednesday on rumors, which it strongly denied, that it had suffered huge derivatives losses. Fuji Bank was the day's most actively traded issue on volume of 17.12 million shares. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du9a018pu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
