\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr falls sharply from earlier highs in Tokyo. \b0\par\par\uc2 504 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 1998\par 03:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      TOKYO, Sept 7 (Reuters) - ASIAN \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ACTIVITY: \par \par \uc2      0010 GMT -- \par \par \uc2      *Dollar down sharply against yen in early Tokyo on active \par \par \uc2  selling led by overseas fund operators. \par \par \uc2      *Greenback earlier rose as high as 134.67 yen on \par \par \uc2  short-covering after U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin \par \par \uc2  reiterated Friday that U.S. dollar policy remained unchanged. \par \par \uc2      *But dollar quickly reversed course as overseas players \par \par \uc2  remained keen to unwind dollar longs. \par \par \uc2      *Dollar/yen at 132.80/90, compared with 133.51/61 in New \par \par \uc2  York Friday. \par \par \uc2  *Some Japanese investors also sold dollars to hedge against further losses, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  *"Morning price moves were a bit exaggerated by thin volume, which is expected to remain thin due to a holiday in the United States," said a dealer at a trust bank. \par \par \uc2  *U.S. markets are closed on Monday for Labor Day. \par \par \uc2  *Weekend meeting between Rubin and Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa provided no surprises, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  *Miyazawa told a news conference in San Francisco on Friday that there had been no discussion of concrete \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels in his 90-minute meeting with Rubin. \par \par \uc2  *But Rubin's comments on dollar invited dollar buy-backs in earlier trade on Monday. \par \par \uc2  *Rubin told a news conference on Friday: "I have said that the strong dollar serves our interests and that we have shared Japanese concerns about the weakness of the yen. Our \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has not changed." \par \par \uc2  *Dealers expect the dollar to remain under pressure amid concerns over Russia and Latin America. \par \par \uc2  *Russia's State Duma will hold a second vote later on Monday on whether to confirm acting Prime Minister Viktor Chernomyrdin in his post. \par \par \uc2  *"Hedge funds are expected to continue unwinding dollar longs amid the deepening turmoil in global financial markets," said a dealer at a trust bank. \par \par \uc2  *"If situations get more chaotic in Latin America and lead to a further weakness in Wall Street stocks, we may see more dollar selling," he added. \par \par \uc2  *On Friday, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was estimated to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market through state-owned Banco do Brasil, selling between $600 million and $1 billion to support the real, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said. \par \par \uc2  *Japan's top financial diplomat Eisuke Sakakibara on Monday continued verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , telling reporters that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the United States are both concerned about "excessive" yen weakness. \par \par \uc2  2305 GMT - \par \par \uc2  *Dollar up but off earlier highs against yen in early Asia. \par \par \uc2  *"As expected, the weekend Rubin-Miyazawa meeting produced nothing concrete on exchange rates. But Rubin's comments on the dollar helped to provide the dollar some support," said a dealer at a city bank. \par \par \uc2  *But dealers said dollar's upside is likely to be capped by Japanese investor selling ahead of half year book closings at end-September. \par \par \uc2  *"Investors are expected to step up dollar selling in the coming weeks to lock in profits and to hedge against losses," said a dealer at a trust bank. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 9780 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. 