\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr jumps more than two yen from low by late Tokyo. \b0\par\par\uc2 623 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 1998\par 09:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab  0636 GMT\tab     TRADED HIGH/LOW AS OF 0600 GMT\par  USD/JPY     135.06/16\tab \tab 135.75-132.95\par  USD/DEM     1.7360/70\tab \tab 1.7435-1.7235\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  --------------------------------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2      TOKYO, Sept 4 (Reuters) - The dollar jumped more than two \par \par \uc2  yen by late Tokyo on Friday from the day's low due to a wave of \par \par \uc2  short-covering and stop-loss bids in volatile trading, driven by \par \par \uc2  a series of comments by monetary officials, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Remarks by Bundesbank President Hans Tietmeyer about the \par \par \uc2  dollar sparked heavy short-covering to lift the greenback \par \par \uc2  decisively above 135 yen in late afternoon, they said. \par \par \uc2  Tietmeyer told Frankfurt business journalists at a dinner on Thursday evening that the recent weakness of the dollar was linked to technical factors such as the expiration of futures and swap contracts and was not linked to fundamental factors. \par \par \uc2  "There are no signs of fundamental weakness here," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rallied to as high as 135.75 yen after hitting stop-loss buy orders around 135 yen, but offers around 135.60-135.80 yen blocked its advance, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The market is short on the dollar. Any dollar-supportive comments should exaggerate the dollar's rise, though it remains under downward pressure," a Japanese city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said U.S. securities firms, who were actively selling dollars in the morning, stood out as main buyers in the afternoon along with Japanese investors such as life insurance firms. \par \par \uc2  In the morning, the greenback briefly fell to a nearly four-month low of 132.95 yen as the market continued selling dollars actively, reflecting its recent downward trend. \par \par \uc2  Yen-supportive remarks by senior officials of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) also encouraged dollar sales in the morning, although the dollar was firmly protected around the low, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Vice Finance Minister Eisuke Sakakibara said on Friday that the yen was bound to strengthen further and investors should be aware of such a risk. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, also told reporters that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at an "appropriate" time. \par \par \uc2  Separately, Haruhiko Kuroda, director-general of MOF's International Bureau, told reporters there was a possibility the yen's weakness could be corrected rapidly. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda also reiterated that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it was "appropriate and timely". \par \par \uc2  Dealers said comments by Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa were partly responsible for inviting dollar short-covering. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa told a regular briefing on Friday he would not comment on a specific level for the dollar/yen rate. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese operators seem to have oversold the dollar in the morning after seeing heavy sales from U.S. fund operators," a senior foreign bank dealer said. "In the morning, many were figuring the dollar to dip below 130 yen. Although the dollar recovered in the afternoon, it's still fragile." \par \par \uc2  An Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also rumoured to have bought dollars heavily on dips around 133 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The market was focusing on a meeting between Miyazawa and U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin in San Francisco scheduled for later on Friday. The two were expected to be joined by Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan. \par \par \uc2  The market was also waiting for the results of U.S. jobs data to see the reaction on Wall Street on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Moody's Investors Service said on Friday it had placed China and Hong Kong on review for possible downgrade, although the impact on dollar/yen was limited, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 134.05/15 yen and 1.7265/75 marks in New York on Thursday. ((Chikafumi Hodo, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021217du9400rr3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
