\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr jumps above 135 yen in Tokyo on Buba comments. \b0\par\par\uc2 359 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 1998\par 07:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      TOKYO, Sept 4 (Reuters) - ASIAN \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ACTIVITY: \par \par \uc2      0524 GMT --*Dollar rebounded sharply in volatile Tokyo \par \par \uc2  afternoon on Friday, shooting up above 135 yen on active \par \par \uc2  short-covering led by Japanese operators. \par \par \uc2      *Comments by Bundesbank President Tietmeyer who said recent \par \par \uc2  weakness of dollar linked to technical factors such as \par \par \uc2  expiration of futures and swap contract and was not linked to \par \par \uc2  fundamentals also spurred active dollar buying across board. \par \par \uc2      *"Short-holders actively bought back and buying intensified \par \par \uc2  after comments by Mr Tietmeyer," a Japanese commercial bank \par \par \uc2  dealer said. \par \par \uc2  *Dollar at 135.34/36 yen compared with 134.05/15 in late New York on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  *Japanese institutional investors were spotted bidding actively along with U.S. securities firms in afternoon. \par \par \uc2  0300 GMT \par \par \uc2  *Dollar pared losses versus yen by midday in Tokyo after falling to a near four-month low on yen-supportive remarks by senior officials of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  *Short-covering helped greenback trim losses as operators cautious about extending positions ahead of potentially market-moving events later on Friday. \par \par \uc2  *Dollar earlier slipped as low as 132.95 yen, its lowest level since May 12, after comments by senior MOF officials Eisuke Sakakibara and Haruhiko Kuroda. \par \par \uc2  *Sakakibara said on Friday yen was bound to strengthen further and investors should be aware of such risk. \par \par \uc2  *Sakakibara, vice minister for international affairs, also told reporters that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at an "appropriate" time. \par \par \uc2  *Separately, Kuroda, director-general of ministry's International Bureau, told reporters there was possibility yen's weakness may be corrected rapidly. \par \par \uc2  *Kuroda also reiterated that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it was "appropriate and timely". \par \par \uc2  *Dealers said dollar-selling intensified after greenback fell below 133.65 yen, low seen after United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars in mid-June. \par \par \uc2  *U.S. fund operators sold dollars aggressively, triggering options-related stop-loss sell orders at around 133.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  *Japanese investors also sold dollars earlier to hedge against further dollar falls. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021217du9400rmg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
