\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar ends U.S. mixed, trims overnight losses. \b0\par\par\uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1998\par 22:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 3 (Reuters) - The dollar ended mixed after heavy overnight selling tapered off in U.S. trade and allowed a modest pullback from near-10-month lows against the mark and Swiss franc and a near-three-month trough against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading remained confined to overseas markets, where Japanese repatriation, equity margin calls and yen carry trade unwinding has dominated flows, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "There was no fresh (dollar) selling out of New York today. Most people just squared up and we may have to wait until Asia and Europe open again before we see more selling," said John Hazelton, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at PNC Bank in Pittsburgh. \par \par \uc2  Global stock markets continued their recent slide, prompting many investors to cash out of long-held dollar stakes to offset their equity market losses overnight. \par \par \uc2  "The driving force in the market continues to be people getting out of positions, not taking new ones. The price action is telling you there is nobody on the other side of those trades," said Marc Chadler, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Deutsche Bank Securities. \par \par \uc2  In addition, Japanese officials reiterated recent threats to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to bolster the yen, triggering dollar sales in Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended lower at 1.7265/75 marks from 1.7275/85 at the open, but up from a session low at 1.7177. The greenback closed well below Wednesday's close at 1.7485/95. \par \par \uc2  The greenback fell to 134.05/15 yen from 135.30/40 at the open and Wednesday's close at 137.70/80. The dollar had traded as low as 133.89 during the session. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said European central banks may have bought dollars for marks near the day low, which gave the greenback a modest lift. But officials gave no indication that they had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite speculation of concern over market stability ahead of monetary union. \par \par \uc2  Russia's economic crisis continued to unnerve investors in the United States and around the world as the threat of spillover to other developing nations raises the prospect of a lengthy global retrenchment. \par \par \uc2  "Our economy is still in great shape and relatively unaffected by events elsewhere in the world. But could that all change? Absolutely," Hazelton said. \par \par \uc2  Moody's Investors Service cut its ratings on Brazil and Venezuela, one day after Colombia devalued its peso. Some economists fear U.S. exports to the region could shrivel if Latin nations fail to avoid Russia's fate. \par \par \uc2  Russia saw its rouble fall to 18 per dollar on Thursday, nearly three times as many roubles as the dollar fetched a few weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen selling dollars in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to bolster the real. \par \par \uc2  Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Haruhiko Kuroda overnight said the yen was correcting from recent weakness and that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any time. \par \par \uc2  His colleague and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat, Eisuke Sakakibara, added that it was risky to have large \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions in current conditions. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen's slide appeared to be gaining momentum, despite an apparent lack of economic fundamentals to sustain it. \par \par \uc2  "One thing I know about dollar/yen-when it's ready to go up, it will go up. When it's trading like it is now, you get out of the way," said Mark Gargano, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives at First Union National Bank. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended little changed at 1.4175/80 Swiss francs from 1.4180/90 at the open, but was well below Wednesday's close at 1.4422/32. Sterling dipped to $1.6758/68 from $1.6808/18, but was still above Wednesday's close at $1.6697/07. \par \par \uc2     The dollar rose to Canadian $1.5380/90 from C$1.5340/50 at \par \par \uc2   the open but was below Wednesday's close at C$1.5424/34. The \par \par \uc2   Australian dollar rose to $0.5855/60 from $0.5760/65.   ((--Chris Stetkiewicz, 212-859-1664)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021217du9300qxj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
