\par\par \b \uc2  OPTIONS - Short-end vols rise as dollar dives. \b0\par\par\uc2 460 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1998\par 13:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 3 (Reuters) - Short-dated implied volatility of dollar options rose on Thursday, as the dollar fell to nine-month lows against the mark and approached two-month lows against the yen, options dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "It's the same old refrain in this market," said Gerome Bono, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2  options at Credit Suisse First Boston. \par \par \uc2  "When the dollar goes down, the options market starts buying." \par \par \uc2  The dollar lost ground as the market remained far from confident about the recovery in the U.S. stock market, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The yen was further buoyed by verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from top-level officials at \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark was trading at 1.7320/25 at 1020 GMT, off earlier nine-month lows of 1.7297, but below the 1.7502/05 level quoted in late European trade on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen dropped to a low of 135.40 and was trading at 135.75/80, from Wednesday's late 137.80/85. \par \par \uc2  Markets were nervous ahead of Friday's meeting of Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa and U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin in San Francisco, with the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen overhanging the market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But increased levels of volatility failed to filter through to the longer end, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "People are afraid because as we get close to 135, a lot of hedge funds are selling spot and selling volatility in one-year," said a trader from a French bank in Paris. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/yen vol rose to 17.10/50 percent, from 16.65/17.05 in late Europe on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  One-year vol was steady at 15.00/40 percent, from 15.05/45. \par \par \uc2  One-month 25 delta risk reversals remained strongly in favour of yen calls, at a high 1.1/1.4 pct, unchanged from Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/mark volatility climbed to 11.40/80 percent from 10.70/11.00 percent late on Wednesday, while one-year vol edged up to 9.45/85 from 9.30/50 percent. \par \par \uc2  Risk reversals favoured mark calls, at 0.65/0.95, from 0.7/0.9 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Mark/yen options trading was active, dealers said, and volatility rose. \par \par \uc2  One-month mark/yen volatility rose to 16.20/60 percent from 15.70/16.30 on Wednesday, while one-year climbed to 14.40/70 percent from 14.10/40. \par \par \uc2  One-month risk reversals favoured mark puts, at 0.7/1.0. \par \par \uc2  Spot mark/yen was trading near session lows at 78.38/41, from 78.68/70 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  ((Carolyn Cohn, London newsroom +44 171 542 6320, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021217du9300q3l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
