\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar slides further vs yen in afternoon Tokyo. \b0\par\par\uc2 408 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1998\par 07:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      TOKYO, Sept 3 (Reuters) - ASIAN \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ACTIVITY: \par \par \uc2      0500 GMT --*Dollar eased further against yen in early \par \par \uc2  afternoon trade after being top-heavy in morning trade. \par \par \uc2      *One trader said there was no fresh news in afternoon and \par \par \uc2  focus on whether dollar would be supported at 136.50 yen. \par \par \uc2     *Clear break below that point may send dollar down to 135 \par \par \uc2  yen again. Dollar quoted at 136.70/80 yen at 0500 GMT. \par \par \uc2     *Dealer at European bank said: "There is a sense that hedge \par \par \uc2  funds are still keen to liquidate dollar/yen positions to make \par \par \uc2  up fall losses in emerging markets, which were again hit, by \par \par \uc2  Colombia's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation and Russian woes.. \par \par \uc2  *"I thought the dollar would recover to 140 yen, but its failure to bounce back even to 139 yen in morning trade made the dollar vulnerable to falls," bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  *Bleak prospects Russian political and economic turmoil would be settled soon and ripple effects on Latin American nations may hit U.S. stock market again. \par \par \uc2  *"Given that economic relations between the United States and Latin American nations are closer than those between Russia and Germany, Colombia's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation and jittery mood of other Latin American nations could work in favour of dollar sales," said European bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  0300 GMT - *Dollar slid against yen by midday Tokyo on Thursday after jawboning by monetary authorities ahead of meeting on Friday of Japan Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa and U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  *Selling accelerated on stop-loss sales and long-liquidation in late morning, prompting the dollar to briefly dip below 137 yen, they said.' \par \par \uc2  *"Remarks by (senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Eisuke) Sakakibara increased perceived downside risks for the dollar," a dealer at a major city bank said. Sakakibara said on Thursday excessive yen weakness was not yet fully corrected. \par \par \uc2  *"Comments by Sakakibara and (Finance Ministry official Haruhiko) Kuroda depressed dollar, but it was already heavy on the topside after failing to rise above 138.50 yen," another city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  *Sakakibara also said it was risky to have large \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions in current conditions, and Japanese investors needed to be concerned about holding foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets. \par \par \uc2  Separately, Kuroda said that if necessary \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  at an appropriate time. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 1396 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du93023ro\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
