\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CROSS-Mark drops vs yen in late Tokyo. \b0\par\par\uc2 387 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1998\par 10:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 3 (Reuters) - The mark edged down against the yen by late Tokyo trade on Thursday, pressured by late rounds of profit-taking by U.S. fund operators for the purpose of covering losses in emerging markets, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The mark rose above 79 yen in early trade, but it later struggled to extend gains as rounds of yen-buying emerged across to the board, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Falls in dollar/yen also indirectly pushed down mark/yen in late trade, they said. \par \par \uc2  As of 0805 GMT, the mark stood at 78.30/33 yen, compared with 78.76 yen in late New York on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Yen-buying was dominant across the board by late trade. Firm support in mark/yen is seen slightly above 78 yen, but a clear drop could spur follow-through sales," a senior foreign bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Lingering worries about emerging markets, stemming from a defacto \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation in Colombia and persisting concerns over the situation in Russia, forced overseas fund operators to lock up profits in mark/yen, they said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yen was bought back heavily against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , along with the Australian dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Aussie dollar dropped sharply to 79.09/19 yen from 80.54/64 in late New York on Wednesday. It rose above 81 yen in the morning. \par \par \uc2  In early trade, short-covering pushed up the mark/yen. \par \par \uc2  But comments from from senior officials at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) triggered falls in dollar/yen and gradually weighed on mark/yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Vice Finance Minister Eisuke Sakakibara said excessive yen weakness had not been fully corrected. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara told reporters: "It is risky to take large \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions in current conditions." \par \par \uc2  Senior MOF official Haruhiko Kuroda told reporters on Thursday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at an appropriate time if necessary. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc was at 95.20/23 yen against 95.48 yen in late New York trade, while sterling was at 227.26/38 yen against 230.07 yen. The Ecu stood at 154.24/29 yen compared with 155.84/89 yen in early Asian trade on Thursday. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du9302305\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
