\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar dips vs yen in Tokyo on Sakakibara, Kuroda. \b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1998\par 03:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      TOKYO, Sept 3 (Reuters) - ASIAN \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ACTIVITY: \par \par \uc2      0050 GMT --Dollar dipped below 138 yen in early Tokyo on \par \par \uc2  Thursday, pressure by comments from from senior officials at \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF). \par \par \uc2      *Dollar dropped to as low as around 137.40 yen after \par \par \uc2  hitting a high of 138.45 yen in early trade. \par \par \uc2      *Senior Finance Ministry official Eisuke Sakakibara said on \par \par \uc2  Thursday that excessive yen weakness not yet fully corrected. \par \par \uc2      *"Comments by Sakakibara and Kuroda depressed the dollar, \par \par \uc2  but it was already heavy on the topside after failing to rise \par \par \uc2  above 138.50 yen," a city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  *Sakakibara also said it is risky to have large \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions in current conditions and Japanese investors need to be concerned about holding foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets. \par \par \uc2  *Separately, senior Finance Ministry official Haruhiko Kuroda said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  at appropriate time if necessary. \par \par \uc2  *As of 0052 GMT, dollar stood at 137.70/75 yen against 137.70/80 in late New York. \par \par \uc2  *Rounds of liquidation and sporadic sales from U.S. fund operators drove down dollar/yen, but follow-through sales losses limited, with firm support seen around 137.00 yen. \par \par \uc2  *One Japanese bank dealer said a medium-sized Japanese life insurer, who was spotted buying actively in Tokyo on Wednesday, was rumoured to have taken profits this morning. \par \par \uc2  *Weakness in Japanese stock market also factor to limit active follow-through sales. At 0055 GMT, key Nikkei average was down 0.88 percent or 125.92 points at 14,250.70. \par \par \uc2  0023 GMT \par \par \uc2  *Dollar well-supported, standing above 138 yen in early Tokyo on Thursday due to short-covering led by interbank operators, but gains limited, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  *Dollar capped around 138.50 yen on sporadic sales by U.S. fund operators. Stop-loss buy orders rumoured placed at 138.50 yen, but follow-through bids limited. \par \par \uc2  2310 GMT \par \par \uc2  *Dollar edged above 138 yen in early Asian trade on Thursday due to follow-through short-covering. \par \par \uc2  *But technical resistance seen capping dollar near Wednesday's New York high of around 138.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  *Short-covering mood strong in early trade, clear break above 138.50 yen could spur upward trend. \par \par \uc2  *"The dollar is still vulnerable as trend in Wall Street remains unclear, but short-covering demand should keep the dollar well-supported in the near term," a Japanese commercial bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  *Dealers said stop-loss buying orders rumoured to be lined up around 138.50 yen. Clear break above that level may pave way to next resistance at 139.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  *Dollar expected to be supported around 137.40/50 yen, with bids by Japanese retail investors expected around 136 yen. \par \par \uc2  *In New York on Wednesday, dollar failed to sustain early gains, ending mostly lower as rebound in U.S. stocks tapered off and dollar buying interest dried up in thin trade. \par \par \uc2  *Market focusing on movements in emerging markets and how they will affect dollar/yen after Colombia announced de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation and Malaysia imposed exchange controls on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  *Dollar-short holders may unwind positions ahead of meeting between Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa and U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin in San Francisco this weekend. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du93022vs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
