\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar slides against yen after Sakakibara. \b0\par\par\uc2 495 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1998\par 06:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab  0300 GMT\tab      TRADED HIGH/LOW AS OF 0300 GMT\par  USD/JPY     137.07/10\tab \tab    138.45-136.85\par  USD/DEM     1.7425/28\tab \tab    1.7513-1.7417\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  --------------------------------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2      TOKYO, Sept 3 (Reuters) - Dollar slid against yen by midday \par \par \uc2  Tokyo on Thursday after jawboning by monetary authorities ahead \par \par \uc2  of meeting on Friday of Japan Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa \par \par \uc2  and U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Selling accelerated on stop-loss sales and long-liquidation \par \par \uc2  in late morning, prompting the dollar to briefly dip below 137 \par \par \uc2  yen, they said. \par \par \uc2  "Remarks by (senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Eisuke) Sakakibara increased perceived downside risks for the dollar," a dealer at a major city bank said. Sakakibara said on Thursday excessive yen weakness was not yet fully corrected. \par \par \uc2  "Comments by Sakakibara and (Finance Ministry official Haruhiko) Kuroda depressed the dollar, but it was already heavy on the topside after failing to rise above 138.50 yen," another city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also said it was risky to have large \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions in current conditions, and Japanese investors needed to be concerned about holding foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets. \par \par \uc2  Separately, Kuroda said that if necessary \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  at an appropriate time. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, speculation about a breakthrough among the ruling and opposition parties for a political solution to support ailing banks buoyed the yen. \par \par \uc2  Tetsuzo Fuyushiba, secretary-general of the opposition New Peace Party, told Reuters Television on Thursday that parliament should discuss measures to deal with troubled banks before they fail, including the use of public funds. \par \par \uc2  Separately, Kosuke Nakahira, a former top Japanese financial diplomat, told Reuters Television that Miyazawa "will demonstrate his firm determination to avoid systemic risk in Japan" when he meets Rubin. \par \par \uc2  On \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Nakahira said that markets should be mindful joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can occur at any time. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets "are not effective if they are not a surprise", he said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said dollar losses were stemmed by bargain-hunting by Japanese importers and interbank players on dips. \par \par \uc2  Military tensions also acted to support the dollar. \par \par \uc2  A senior Japanese government spokesman said on Thursday that the government had information North Korea was preparing to launch another missile. \par \par \uc2  Muneo Suzuki, a deputy chief cabinet secretary, told reporters: "The government has information from various sources that there are preparations under way for a second North Korean missile." \par \par \uc2  Dealers expected dollar to remain under downward pressure on continuing turmoil in emerging markets, after Colombia announced de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation and Malaysia imposed exchange controls on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar also remains vulnerable to stock price movements. \par \par \uc2  S&P 500 stock futures on Globex were lower in Tokyo, raising some concerns over trade on Wall Street later in the day. The key September contract was down 2.70 points at 988.10 as of 0358 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Dollar closed at 137.70/80 yen and 1.7485/95 marks in New York on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du93022h3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
