\par\par \b \uc2  U.S. T-bond steady in Tokyo after giving up gains. \b0\par\par\uc2 412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1998\par 05:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 3 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasuries were steady at midday in Tokyo on Thursday, after giving up earlier gains following the dollar's slide, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "There were some follow-through bids in the long bond earlier as the New York Dow dipped into negative territory shortly before its close," said a dealer at a U.S. brokerage. \par \par \uc2  But the gains were trimmed after the dollar slipped on remarks by senior officials of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Eisuke Sakakibara, Japan's top financial diplomat, told reporters that under current conditions it was risky to have large \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also said Japanese investors should be concerned about the risks of holding foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated assets. \par \par \uc2  Separately, Haruhiko Kuroda, director-general of the ministry's international bureau, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at the appropriate time if and when necessary. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 137.00/10 yen at midday in Tokyo, compared with an earlier high of 138.45 yen. \par \par \uc2  "Treasuries tracked the dollar/yen's moves this morning. But people were reluctant to trade aggressively before the key events on Friday," said a dealer at a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  Important events on Friday include the release of U.S. jobs data for August, a meeting between U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin and Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa, and a speech by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan. \par \par \uc2  "Greenspan's speech is on Friday evening and U.S. markets are closed on Monday for Labor Day," said a dealer at a U.S. brokerage. "It is too risky to have positions, short or long, over this long U.S. weekend." \par \par \uc2  The benchmark 30-year Treasury bond was at 102-14/32 at midday in Tokyo, unchanged from its level in New York late on Wednesday. Its yield stood at 5.34 percent. \par \par \uc2  U.S. economic data due out later on Thursday, - weekly jobless claims at 1230 GMT and the NAPM non-manufacturing data for August at 1400 GMT - are unlikely to be market-movers, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Also on Thursday, San Francisco Federal Reserve President Robert Parry will speak at Community and Business Leaders luncheon in Boise, Idaho, at 1800 GMT. \par \par \uc2  September T-bond futures were at 126-27/32, up 4/32 from the previous close. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 9780 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du93022a7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
