\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr erases losses vs yen on Nikkei,JGB yield drops. \b0\par\par\uc2 584 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 1998\par 02:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      TOKYO, Sept 1 (Reuters) - ASIAN \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ACTIVITY: \par \par \uc2      0018 GMT -- \par \par \uc2      *Dollar stood well above its earlier low against yen in \par \par \uc2  early Tokyo, lifted by short-covering and buying by Japanese \par \par \uc2  importers. \par \par \uc2      *Dollar buying related to setting up of fresh investment \par \par \uc2  trust funds also detected. \par \par \uc2      *Weak Tokyo stocks and falling Japanese government bond \par \par \uc2  (JGB) yields also weighed on yen. \par \par \uc2      *Dollar at 139.55/65 yen, off earlier low of 138.70 yen. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark at 1.7477/82, 1.7480/85 in New York. \par \par \uc2  *Nikkei 225 stock average down 2.35 percent at 13,776.12. Yield of benchmark 10-year JGB fell to record low of 0.995 percent. \par \par \uc2  *But dealers said dollar's rise likely to be checked by selling interest from Japanese institutional investors. \par \par \uc2  *"Japanese investors are likely to sell foreign securities to lock in profits before mid-year bookclosings (at end-September). Some are also looking to sell dollars for hedging purposes," said a dealer at a trust bank. \par \par \uc2  *Worries about New York stocks are also likely to limit dollar's upside, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  *New York's Dow Jones industrial fell 512.61 points at 7,539.07, its second-biggest point loss ever. \par \par \uc2  *"Unwinding of yen carry trades by hedge funds is likely to continue amid the ongoing global stock market turmoil," said a dealer at a city bank. \par \par \uc2  *Yen carry trade is a strategy in which fund operators borrow low-cost yen funds and convert them into dollars through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rate swaps. \par \par \uc2  *"So I think there is still a chance for the dollar to visit the recent low of 133.65 yen seen after the U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (in mid-June)," the city bank dealer added. \par \par \uc2  *Senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Haruhiko Kuroda said on Tuesday that a correction of excessively strong dollar/weak yen has begun and will continue. \par \par \uc2  *Kuroda also told reporters that there was a possibility that the dollar would weaken rapidly and said institutional and other investors should take care with regard to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks. \par \par \uc2  2307 GMT - \par \par \uc2  *Dollar slipped against yen in early Asia after ending sharply lower overnight following a 512-point drop in New York's Dow Jones industrial index. \par \par \uc2  *Dollar seen remaining vulnerable, but expectations that Tokyo shares will open sharply down limiting yen's gains. \par \par \uc2  *Dollar at 139.20/30 yen after briefly slipping below 139 yen earlier. Dollar/yen closed at 139.64/69 in New York. \par \par \uc2  *"The Nikkei is expected to start weaker after a sharp drop in CME Nikkei futures overnight. But its impact on dollar/yen might not be so great because the market is currently focused on the dollar's vulnerability," said a dealer at a trust bank. \par \par \uc2  *Stop-loss dollar selling could emerge if dollar falls decisively below 138.50 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  *Following Wall Street's second-largest point drop, U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said on Monday he was in close touch with other Group of Seven nations as well as Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan and President Bill Clinton. He urged Japan to take necessary steps to revive its economy. \par \par \uc2  *Market will be carefully watching President Clinton's two-day visit to Russia from Tuesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  *On Monday, Clinton urged Russia to pursue market reforms, saying a return to a Soviet-style centrally planned economy was bound to fail. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 9780 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du9101x0w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
