\par\par \b \uc2  Take the dollar and run? No thanks, investors say. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 1998\par 15:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 28 (Reuters) - World markets may be going wild, but for once the traditional safe haven of the U.S. dollar offers little comfort, analysts said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The exposure of the world's leading economy to ailing Latin American markets, the possibility of impeachment for President Bill Clinton and a need among speculators to take profits in dollars to counteract losses in Russia have all put pressure on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent days. \par \par \uc2  There is, however, one exception. For a rock-steady position in these volatile times, try the dollar/mark exchange rate, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Germany has Russia, the U.S. has Latin America," said Paul Meggyesi, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank. "Dollar/mark is a balance of these two risks." \par \par \uc2  Investors have sold the mark heavily in the past two weeks as Russia has descended into political and financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  Germany's proximity to and banking links with Russia have made investors wary of the mark. But they have chosen to sell for sterling or the Swiss franc, seen as less exposed to problems in emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  As a result, dollar/mark has moved in a two-pfennig range over the past fortnight and is likely to stay that way, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "People have exaggerated the extent to which Germany has been affected by Russia. The banking sector is not nearly as exposed as people think," said Teis Knuthsen, head of research at SE-Banken. \par \par \uc2  "But if Latin America were to fail, it would be very substantial for the U.S. from a banking and trade perspective." \par \par \uc2  The simmering saga of Clinton's affair with former White House intern Monica Lewinsky is also taking its toll on the dollar, analysts added. \par \par \uc2  "The emotional influences for dollar/mark on the upside are the Russian and Asian crises. The downside emotional factors are Clinton/Lewinsky and the potential for a Latin American meltdown," said Chris Furness, senior market strategist at 4CAST. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has failed to make headway against the yen in the past few weeks, despite recession in Japan, the Nikkei stock index at 12-year lows on Friday and weakness in other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dollar/yen fell to a five-week low of 140.35. \par \par \uc2  The main factor in dollar/yen's inability to drive higher has been heavy selling by hedge funds burnt by losses in Russia, dealers and analysts said. \par \par \uc2  They said hedge funds, which work on the basis of margin payments, have had to top up their initial margin deposits as they make losses on positions in emerging markets. To fund those top-up payments, they have sold marks and dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  "This mechanism seems to have worked best in dollar/yen," said Klaus Kusber, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson. "It was very, very surprising the way dollar/yen dropped back below 142 despite the weakness in the Nikkei and other Japanese fundamentals." \par \par \uc2  The wisdom of buying yen has also been reinforced by comments in support of the yen by top Japanese officials. \par \par \uc2  The markets are all too aware of the possibility of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week to support \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ranging from the Australian dollar to the Danish crown. \par \par \uc2  Dealers see the long shadow of European central banks overhanging dollar/mark. \par \par \uc2  Central banks have offered to sell dollars for marks on any rises in the past weeks, traders said, and they saw this as an attempt to avoid volatility in the mark before the start of Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  next year. \par \par \uc2  "You get these stories about central banks being in, when they are just involved in commercial transactions," said Michael Metcalfe, treasury economist at NatWest Global Financial Markets. \par \par \uc2  "But it would be in their interests to keep dollar/mark steady in the current environment." \par \par \uc2  ((London newsroom +44 171 542 6320, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8s01sqw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
