\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen capitalises on Russia sell-off. \b0\par\par\uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 1998\par 17:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 27 (Reuters) - Deepening concern over Russia drove the mark and commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower on Thursday, to the benefit of the yen, analysts and dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Investors sold the mark and dollar to buy the yen, in a reversal of recent trades, in order to offset losses in Russia and other emerging markets, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Hedge funds have been selling mark/yen," said a trader from a U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  Russia failed to restore confidence in its markets on Thursday, after disappointing foreign investors with its debt restructuring package released late on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Moscow Interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Exchange (MICEX) suspended all rouble \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade on Thursday and said trading would not resume on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Today we haven't really seen any new information hit the market," said Stewart Newnham, international economist at First Chicago. "There's been a lot of speculation of course." \par \par \uc2  A Finance Ministry official said details of Russia's short-term rouble debt restructuring plan were likely to be announced on Friday. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said only hard political news on Russia could calm feverish markets. \par \par \uc2  "The political situation is still pretty volatile," said Patrick Foley, senior economic adviser at Lloyds Bank. \par \par \uc2  Rumours of President Yeltsin's resignation, later denied, were among factors hitting the mark on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The mark was trading at 78.92/95 yen at 1504 GMT, from a one-month low of 78.67 and from 79.92/95 in late European trade on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also lost ground to the yen, falling to a one-week low of 142.20. It was trading at 142.73/83, from 144.37/42 in late European trade on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also helped the yen on Thursday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Vice Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara said he was watching moves in mark/yen carefully. \par \par \uc2  He also said it was important for Japanese investors to lock in profits in any foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  transaction. \par \par \uc2  The mark fell to six-week lows against the pound and 10-week lows against the Swiss franc, both safe havens from emerging market woes. \par \par \uc2  Commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meanwhile, felt the full thrust of falling commodity prices, amid fears that Russia would try to increase oil and precious metal output against a backdrop of declining global demand for commodities. \par \par \uc2  Central banks pulled their weight to support the Canadian and Australian dollars, which fell to record lows. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the Bank of Canada in the European morning to defend the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada later raised interest rates by 100 basis points, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly to rescue the Canadian dollar from a record low of C$1.5845 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Federal Reserve, meanwhile, sold dollars for Australian dollars, dealers said, hauling the Australian dollar back above lows below $0.5500. \par \par \uc2  The New Zealand dollar also hit record lows below $0.4800. \par \par \uc2  Oil-dependent Norway's crown fell to record lows against the mark and against its Ecu index. \par \par \uc2  But the Norwegian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had not changed policy since Monday, when it said it was suspending use of interest rates to keep the crown steady against the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  unit, and scaling back \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "for the time being". \par \par \uc2  The dollar reached six-week highs of 1.8135 marks, but dipped to 1.8070/80 by 1527 GMT, from 1.8062/72 in late Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar failed to make further gains because of its exposure to ailing markets in Latin America, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is not a safe haven from a global emerging market crisis," said Paul Meggyesi, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Deutsche Bank. \par \par \uc2  ((Carolyn Cohn, London newsroom +44 171 542 6320, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8r01mlt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
