\par\par \b \uc2  OPTIONS - Dlr/mark vols gain, dlr/yen vols cool. \b0\par\par\uc2 407 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 21:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, Aug 26 (Reuters) - Implied options volatilities were slightly firmer in dollar/mark as the Russian rouble drama continued to unfold Wednesday but were softer in dollar/yen as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears receded, traders and analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Front-end dollar/mark volatility was holding gains made during European trade when spot pushed through 1.8030 marks, they said. \par \par \uc2  With dollar/mark hovering just over 1.8050 marks, one-month volatility stood at 8.25-percent midpoint, up from 8.0 percent late Tuesday. The one-year was little changed from the previous session at 8.7/8.8 percent. \par \par \uc2  "The dealing has been back and forth. There's not a lot of volatility movement. It happened overnight," said MaryBeth Slack, senior options analyst for Thomson Global Markets. \par \par \uc2  Mounting fears Russia would default on debt obligations bolstered the dollar, though key resistance at 1.81 marks remained intact. \par \par \uc2  "We need a break of 1.81 (marks) to see vols get much higher," said a Wall Street-based options broker. \par \par \uc2  Risk reversals, the put-call pricing differential, were hanging onto a slight mark call preference at flat/0.15 in the one-month as the dollar strengthened. \par \par \uc2  The rouble fell more than 40 percent against the mark overnight, and the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it could no longer \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Rouble/dollar trade was suspended for the day. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen volatility softened for a second day after Japanese officials appeared to tone down \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rhetoric earlier this week and the spot range narrowed. \par \par \uc2  "They pushed dollar/yen volatility down quite a bit overnight. There has been a bit of a policy shift," said Slack. \par \par \uc2  One-month volatility shed about 0.6 percentage point early on overnight dollar firmness, but has regained about half of that decline to trade at 15.3/15.6 percent as the dollar backtracked toward 144 yen in the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Risk reversals stayed biased for yen calls at 0.4/0.6 in the one-month despite the reduced focus on gauging Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  intentions, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The short end still seems to be quite perky for yen calls," said the Wall Street broker. \par \par \uc2  He noted one-year options have retained a sharp yen put bias at 1.0/1.15 for the puts. \par \par \uc2  ((Susan Kelly, Chicago Derivatives Desk 312-408-8750 chicago.derivatives.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8q01jlv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
