\par\par \b \uc2  Spot HK dollar trade in Tokyo up sharply in July. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tamawa Kadoya \par \uc2 561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 07:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 26 (Reuters) - Speculation over China's monetary policy in July fueled a sharp rise in spot dollar/Hong Kong dollar trading in Tokyo, excluding electronic brokering, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Hong Kong dollar trade has risen in Tokyo mainly from speculative operators' moves over the possibility of China devaluing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a dealer at a U.S. bank said. \par \par \uc2  Spot transactions for July in dollar/Hong Kong dollar stood at $252 million, compared to $94 million in the same month a year ago, according to data from the Money Brokers Association in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The rise continued a year-on-year jump, with spot trade in dollar/Hong Kong dollar standing at $1.992 trillion in the January-July period, up sharply from $857 million for the same period in 1997, the data showed. \par \par \uc2  Concerns that China may devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Hong Kong abandon its peg to the U.S. dollar flared as the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  scraped near eight-year lows against the dollar in mid-June. \par \par \uc2  Joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the United States in mid-June temporarily eased pressure on China, but even if they do so again, such action is not seen as an instant remedy to soothe the situation in China, they said. \par \par \uc2  China has persistently denied it would devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Chinese Foreign Trade Minister Shi Guangsheng has pledged the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would remain stable but said Beijing was paying a heavy price for its commitment not to devalue, the China Daily reported on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Liu Mingkang, deputy governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the People's Bank of China, said on Aug 11 that: "The renminbi (yuan) does not need to be devalued and will not be devalued." \par \par \uc2  In early August, U.S. fund operators attacked Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stocks, prompting the yen to hit eight-year lows. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong has attempted to ward off attacks as monetary authorities stepped in to support the stock and futures markets. \par \par \uc2  Joseph Yam, chief executive of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in a letter to the Financial Times newspaper on August 17, vowed to tackle \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market. \par \par \uc2  Recently, such speculators were seen unwinding Hong Kong-dollar forwards and taking profits in dollar/yen to cover losses in emerging markets, including Russia, traders said. \par \par \uc2  But continued speculation and uncertainties in emerging markets will likely keep trade in the Hong Kong dollar in Tokyo active, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Speculative operators are also expected to continue seeking profits as trade in major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as dollar/yen and dollar/mark has stalemated, a dealer at a U.S. securities house said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Japanese bankers said that hedging needs by Japanese trading houses and banks will also boost Hong Kong dollar trade in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "As Japan has made major investments in Hong Kong, they will be forced to hedge against such investments," a dealer at a major Japanese commercial bank said. \par \par \uc2  On a quarterly basis, trade in dollar/Hong Kong dollar stood at $770 million in April-June 1998, compared with $304 million in the same period in 1997, and $970 million in January-March 1998 versus $459 million for the same period in 1997. \par \par \uc2  ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8027 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8q01lgy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
