\par\par \b \uc2  OPTIONS - Dlr/mark vols rise on safe-haven buying. \b0\par\par\uc2 530 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 14:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 26 (Reuters) - Implied volatilities for dollar/mark options rose on Wednesday following a stronger dollar in the spot market as investors ran for safe-haven cover in the wake of financial turmoil in Russia. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/mark options spiked up to 8.20/8.40 percent from 7.90/8.30 percent in late Europe on Tuesday as the dollar topped highs around 1.8074 marks in the wake of comments about Russia by German Finance Minister Theo Waigel. \par \par \uc2  "Most of the movements are really on safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Satu Jaatinen, vice president of risk advisory at Chase Manhattan Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Waigel said it was not possible for the Group of Seven (G7) industrialised nations, the International Monetary Fund (IMF) or the European Union (EU) to fix Russia's problems. \par \par \uc2  Those comments further unsettled financial markets, which expressed disappointed over a debt restructuring package announced by the Russian government late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Russian rouble dropped by more than a third on Wednesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  voided dollar trades and would not set a dollar rate for the day. \par \par \uc2  At 1130 GMT dollar/mark had settled back from its highs to around 1.8060/67, but was still sharply up compared with 1.7974/84 in late European trading on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Six-month dollar/mark vols were trading at 8.50/8.75 percent compared with 8.40/8.60 percent, while one-year vols were trading at 8.60/8.90 percent compared with 8.50/8.70 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark risk/reversals were quoted in favour of mark calls at -0.05/0.2 compared with mark calls favoured on Tuesday at 0.05/0.35. \par \par \uc2  Jaatinen also said most commodity-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were also under fire as the result of weaker oil and commodities prices. \par \par \uc2  "Oil prices are down, gold prices are down and those countries which are dependent upon commodities are hurting most," she said. \par \par \uc2  One-month mark/Norwegian crown vols rose to 9.00/9.75 percent compared to around 6.50/7.25 percent earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  Traders said dollar/yen vols slipped as speculation dwindled that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to dampen dollar/yen during thin markets on Monday's bank holiday in London. "They have certainly been known to try strategies like that in the distant past, but they haven't done anything like that in a good long time, so we have no particular reason to think they'll do it this time," said Gerome Bono, London head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Options at Credit Suisse First Boston. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was trading at 144.48/58 compared with 144.82/92 in late European trading on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/yen vols slipped to 15.10/40 compared with 15.70/16.00 in Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/yen risk/reversals were trading in favour of yen calls at 0.35/0.65 compared with 0.35/0.65 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, paul.casciato@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8q01kt9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
