\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Crisis/Glance: Japan Bk Stks Dn On Reform Delay Fears \b0\par\par\uc2 423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 10:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  -- Japanese opposition parties and the ruling LDP will resume Parliamentary debate on banking reform, a main opposition Democratic Party official said. \par \par \uc2  -- Shares of Japan's top 19 banks fell, with 10 of them sinking to new year-to-date lows, on gloom about the possibility that legislative bills to clean up banks' bad loans may get stuck in parliament. News of the agreement to resume debate didn't stop the shares' slide. \par \par \uc2  -- Continuing uncertainty over the Japanese government's attempts to reform the banking sector damped market sentiment; Tokyo stocks ended 1.4% lower, with the Nikkei down 206.90 points at 14,866.03. \par \par \uc2  -- The dollar rose against the yen in Tokyo due to uncertainty over the fate of legislation to shore up \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's financial system and growing confidence that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost the yen is not imminent. At 0800 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at Y144.73, up from Y144.32 late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  -- Japanese Finance Ministry's international bureau head Kuroda said the ministry's policy on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unchanged and noted Finance Minister Miyazawa said the government would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when necessary. \par \par \uc2  -- Simple monetary expansion could remedy \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic ills, said U.S. economist Krugman at an Asian Business Forecast conference in Singapore, noting that "Japan needs a weak yen." \par \par \uc2  -- China's Foreign Trade Minister Shi told French Finance Minister Strauss-Kahn the yuan won't be devalued but Japan is being economically destabilizing to the region, according to a report. China and the G-7 are "very close" in their concerns about Japan, Strauss-Kahn said. \par \par \uc2  -- The IMF's executive board approved the next $1 billion credit tranche for Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  -- Hong Kong government officials met with fund managers to explain the government's new policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market to drive out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators, according to a fund manager at AIG Investment Corp. \par \par \uc2  -- South Korea's economy will shrink around 5% in the first half of 1998 as a result of extremely weak corporate investment and consumption, \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea Governor Chon said. \par \par \uc2  -- South Korea's Hyundai Motor Co. resumed production after a month-long strike, but may have to halt its auto-assembly lines again because of a lack of parts. \par \par \uc2  -- The Australian dollar was weaker late, but stalled tentatively following its slide to a new 12-year low at US$0.5730 earlier in the session. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 26-08-98 \par \par \uc2  0820GMT \par \par \uc2  1042 4190 55126 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8q079lh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
