\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Roundup -2: \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Verbal  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Loses Impact \b0\par\par\uc2 362 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 08:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The mark is quoted at Y80.20, up from Y80.16 in New York late Tuesday but below Y80.58 in London late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling is at $1.6377, down from $1.6395 in New York late Tuesday. Sterling was at $1.6398 in London late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Japanese opposition parties, strongly critical of a government-led merger plan between troubled Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan and Sumitomo Trust & Banking, boycotted the committee meeting because the LDP wouldn't heed its demands that the heads of the two banks testify before the committee. \par \par \uc2  Under the compromise, the committee will resume on Thursday to discuss the LTCB issue, and then discuss the LDP's bills for reforming the banking system on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "It's not as if this is a major yen-selling factor, but from a macroeconomic standpoint, every delay makes us less willing to buy the yen," said a Japanese bank trader. "It's certainly not helping the stock market." \par \par \uc2  Led by a sell-off in the banking sector, the Nikkei stock average ended the day down 206.90 points at 14,866.03. \par \par \uc2  While the dollar could well run into resistance around Y145.00, large sell orders near that level overnight didn't take the dollar down very far, traders said. The dollar's downside support is aided by sentiment that the dire warnings by Japanese officials over the past couple of weeks that they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help the yen are losing some impact. \par \par \uc2  Traders are taking comfort from Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa's comments Tuesday, when he downplayed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless market moves are "disruptive." As a result, the market ignored comments Wednesday from Haruhiko Kuroda, who heads the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international bureau, that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was unchanged. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It's one thing to talk about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  when there is some policy\par move to back it up. But with the financial bills looking delayed,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at this point would have little impact," the Japanese bank\par trader said.\par By John Brinsley; 813-5255-2936; E-MAIL jbrinsley@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 26-08-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0630GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8q079e1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
