\par\par \b \uc2  Yen treks lower, market focus on Russia debt plan. \b0\par\par\uc2 598 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 1998\par 10:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 25 (Reuters) - The dollar was upbeat against the yen in Europe on Tuesday as expectations of yen-boosting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  faded, while it drifted against the mark as the market awaited news on Russia's debt restructuring. \par \par \uc2  The yen was sold off against the dollar and mark after Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said overnight that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there was any market disruption, but then added that he saw no such disruption now. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was at 144.67/77 at 0740 GMT, just off its earlier high, against 143.80/90 in late Europe on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Traders and analysts said Miyazawa's words had encouraged yen bears to sell off the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which in recent days had been buoyed as expectations of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  offset \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's depressed economic situation. \par \par \uc2  "They (the Japanese authorities) have been saying nearly every day that they were about to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and today they're saying 'actually, we wouldn't in these conditions'," said Francis Breedon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Lehman Brothers. "We still need to see how much of an effect we get from this comment." \par \par \uc2  The focus of attention for the market, however, was firmly on Russia, where authorities were scheduled to announce terms for converting short-term debt into longer-term paper to make it easier to service. \par \par \uc2  The dollar and mark were fairly stable as the market awaited new developments, having digested the dismissal of Russia's five-month-old government on Sunday, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark was quoted at 1.7971/78 after trading in a range of just over half a pfennig overnight, against 1.7946/51 in late Europe on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are much of a topic of interest right now," said Glenn Davies, chief economist at Credit Lyonnais Laing. "It's still a bit unclear how some of the developments (in Russian and Latin American financial markets) are going to affect the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  In Russia, trade in the rouble was suspended on Tuesday morning after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slid against the dollar under renewed pressure. In Latin America overnight, debt markets were mixed but calmer after being roiled last week by fears of devaluation. \par \par \uc2  "Potentially, Latin America can have a much bigger impact on the U.S. than Russia can have on Germany... The trade flows are so much bigger between the U.S. and Latin America," said Davies. \par \par \uc2  With the market having had plenty of time to mull over what it could expect from the Russian debt announcement, originally scheduled for Monday, there was little traders could do until the actual event. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like it's going to be a wait-and-see day regarding Russia," said Breedon at Lehman. \par \par \uc2  "Figures do show that the talk about German exposure to Russia is pretty overblown and most of it is guaranteed by the government, so it's not of great macro significance," Breedon said. \par \par \uc2  The Norwegian crown dropped to a new 2-1/2-year low of 4.3640 crowns per mark. This follows the Norwegian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's statement on Monday that it would not change interest rates nor \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a "large scale" for the time being after jacking up interest rates by a total of 250 basis points on Friday and Monday. \par \par \uc2  The crown has been pulled down by sliding oil market prices and fears that Norway's economy was overheating. \par \par \uc2  ((Chizu Nomiyama, London newsroom 44 171 542 6721, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8p01i2v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
