\par\par \b \uc2  OPTIONS - Mark/Norway crown vols held high by spot. \b0\par\par\uc2 512 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 1998\par 14:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 25 (Reuters) - Implied volatilities for mark/Norwegian crown options remained high on Tuesday as the cross rose to a new 2-1/2-year high around 4.3660, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said one-week mark/Norwegian vols remained strongly bid around 9.00/9.25 percent, nearly unchanged from Monday's levels. \par \par \uc2  "The vol is still pretty well bidin the one-week area and the interest isn't confined there," said one dealer at a major European firm. "People are trying to buy mark calls, mark puts, anything they can get their hands on." \par \par \uc2  Vols were bid up as the mark rose to its highest levels since March 1996 against the Norwegian crown, which has been suffering from a slump in oil prices and fears that the Norwegian economy may be overheating. \par \par \uc2  This rise in mark/Norwegian crown followed the Norwegian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision on Monday to raise interest rates for the second time in as many trading days to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Norges Bank then said it would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on a large scale to defend the crown and ruled out further rate rises "for the time-being". \par \par \uc2  The rate hike of 100 basis points on Monday was the seventh tightening since March, and followed a 150-basis-point rise in the same rates on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that after Monday's announcement, one-month mark/Norwegian crown vols rose to 7.00 percent from 6.5 percent at midday in Europe on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Options dealers said the market has been relatively sedate outside of the Scandinavian interest, with some slippage in dollar/yen vols in spite of a firmer U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/yen vols were at 15.50/16.00 percent compared with 16.00/16.40 percent in late Europe on Monday, while one-year vols slipped to 15.90/16.40 percent compared with 16.10/16.40 percent. \par \par \uc2  At 1140 GMT dollar/yen was trading at 144.51/56 compared with 143.80/90 in late European trading on Monday. \par \par \uc2  But some dealers said they expected dollar/yen vols to rise again on growing speculation over Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the August bank holiday in London on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "With our holiday on Monday there is the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so I would be a little bit worried," said another dealer at a European bank. "I would be very reluctant to sell vols anywhere at the moment." \par \par \uc2  One-month risk reversals in dollar/yen favoured yen calls at 0.35/0.65 compared with favouring yen calls by 0.3/0.6 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark vols were quiet on Monday, mirroring a steady spot market which saw the dollar edge up slightly. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark was trading around 1.7966/71 compared with 1.7946/51 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, paul.casciato@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8p01g1g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
