\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr eases vs mark, subdued vs yen at Europe midday. \b0\par\par\uc2 652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 1998\par 13:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 24 (Reuters) - The dollar gave up some ground against the mark in Europe, with the market digesting the weekend dismissal of the Russian government and looking towards Moscow's plans to restructure debt. \par \par \uc2  A Russian spokesman said the announcement about a conversion of short-term debt for longer-term paper was expected later on Monday, and traders said there was little the market could do until it saw these plans. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 1.7972/77 marks at 1030 GMT, off a session high of 1.8030 and against 1.7955/65 in late Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "We see blips in dollar/mark now and then when there's bad news out of Russia but they're not particularly long-lasting," said James Mitchell, senior strategist at Nomura International. \par \par \uc2  "If you look at the actual exposure of Germany (to Russia) in terms of bank lending and so on, it's larger than that of the U.S. but it's still not vast," he said. \par \par \uc2  Major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had shrugged off Yeltsin's nomination of Viktor Chernomyrdin as new prime minister and did not show much reaction when Yeltsin effectively named him heir apparent in the next presidential election. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark has been stuck in a range of little over half a pfennig all session. While mark/yen has moved in a larger range of about half a yen, traders said this was due in part in movements in dollar/yen. \par \par \uc2  However, analysts SAID that while the impact of Russia on the dollar and mark was not very marked now, major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could not afford to ignore developments in Moscow. \par \par \uc2  "Russia may not account for a major part of Germany's trade," said Paul McNamara, emerging markets strategist at Julius Baer Investments. \par \par \uc2  "But if you think about the connection with eastern Europe as a whole, you are talking about a source of growth for Germany," McNamara added. \par \par \uc2  And while the dollar is still seen as a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the market is not very tempted to buy it now as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has problems of its own, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Now that Latin America has been brought into the equation and the general emerging market chaos has been associated with problems in the Dow as well, it's clearly not dollar-positive," said Ian Gunner, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had a soft tone against the yen as long-position liquidation continued in the pair with some incentive from fears of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was at 144.41/46, up from its session low of 144.03 but down from 144.93/98 in late Europe Friday. \par \par \uc2  A two-percent drop in Tokyo stock prices, pulled down by share market weakness around the world, had given dollar/yen a boost early in the session. \par \par \uc2  There were some niggling fears about the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  yen-buying following some comments from Japanese Vice Finance Minister Sakakibara. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said in an interview with the Wall Street Journal that Tokyo could be close to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Noting that sentiment about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy may be bottoming out, Sakakibara said it "makes sense" to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Norway's central Norges Bank continued its battle with speculators as they drove the Norwegian crown to its lowest in nearly two years against the mark. \par \par \uc2  Norges Bank, which has a mandate to stabilise the crown, raised key interest rates by 100 basis points on Monday, following a hike of 150 basis points on Friday. It said it would take no further measures. \par \par \uc2  The crown slumped to a low of 4.3590 per mark, but had recovered to 4.3375/95, though this was still weaker than late Friday levels of 4.2885/05. \par \par \uc2  ((Chizu Nomiyama, London newsroom 44 171 542 6721, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8o01eju\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
