\par\par \b \uc2  Top Finance Official Says  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  May  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  To Help Weakening Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Bussey, Staff Reporter of The Wall Street Journal \par \uc2 672 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 1998\par 07:26\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A top Japanese finance official said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets have been "too pessimistic" about the weakening yen, and that Tokyo may be close to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Eisuke Sakakibara, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's vice finance minister for international affairs and the country's most influential spokesman on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, said that sentiment about Japan's ailing economy may be bottoming out. "Given that situation," he said, "it makes sense to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If we are right, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be effective. That's why Mr. Miyazawa and I have been making the comments we've been making," he said, referring to Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa. "Our perception is that the wind is about to shift." He added that "\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is one of the effective instruments we have." \par \par \uc2  Speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets has sent the yen gyrating in recent days, spurred on by the comments of Messrs. Sakakibara and Miyazawa, who have both called for a stronger yen. Asked if this is just a strategy to jawbone the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher, particularly since many independent economists are still negative about Japan's economy, Mr. Sakakibara said: "You cannot just talk. The market has been too pessimistic. When the sentiment is about to change," that's the most effective time to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yen closed in New York trading Friday at 144.78 to the dollar, up 1.64 yen from the close Thursday. At midday Monday in Tokyo, the dollar was at 145 yen. While a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can help Japanese exporters by making their products cheaper abroad, it also can hamper Japanese manufacturers who have moved a large chunk of their production base to other countries. For them, the weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes it more expensive to buy raw materials for their factories. Japan has also been under pressure from the West and other Asian nations to bolster its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because a weaker yen could ignite a new round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations throughout Asia. \par \par \uc2  While Mr. Sakakibara wouldn't say what the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  considers a healthy value for the yen, surveys in Japan show that manufacturers would like to see the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  between 120 yen and 130 yen to the dollar. Tokyo has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice this year -- once in the spring on its own and jointly with the U.S. Treasury in July -- but both moves resulted only in a temporary strengthening of the yen. Indeed, Japan's relentless economic stagnation has soured world markets on the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Mr. Sakakibara said he considered "reasonable" a forecast by the government that Japan's gross domestic product would be roughly flat in fiscal year 1998, ranging somewhere between half a percentage point contraction to half a percentage point growth. But he insisted that large government-spending programs in the pipeline will kick-start the economy beginning in September or October. "I'm sure that in the fourth calendar quarter 1998, there will be growth," he said. "We're hoping that will be the start of the rebound of the Japanese economy." \par \par \uc2  Reminded that Japan has said this repeatedly in the past, Mr. Sakakibara said, "I admit that our forecasts were wrong." But he said he believes there is a new political consensus in Japan to move quickly on the economy, and to resolve the nation's banking crisis. \par \par \uc2  For more than six years, the nation's banks have been hobbled by huge portfolios of nonperforming loans, the legacy of profligate lending in the 1980s. Political wrangling in the years since, along with a lack of consensus about the urgency of the matter, has exacerbated the problem. In recent months, however, the government has fielded several proposals that would begin to address the banks' travails. \par \par \uc2  "It doesn't make sense to criticize Japan for dragging our feet," Mr. Sakakibara said. "The process is quite speedy and will accelerate." \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du8o0165j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
