\par\par \b \uc2  OPTIONS - Dollar vols end firm after stocks swoon. \b0\par\par\uc2 440 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 1998\par 23:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, Aug 21 (Reuters) - Options implied volatilities for the major dollar pairs ended firmer in U.S. trade Friday after mounting fear of an emerging market meltdown sparked a nearly 300-point slide in the Dow Jones Industrial Average. \par \par \uc2  Worries over Russia's economic problems and a domino effect in Latin America gripped the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as well, sending the dollar on a choppy path and supporting volatilities in defiance of weekend time-decay pressure. \par \par \uc2  "There's a lot of interest in overnight options for the weekend. Nobody's selling the front end, even in dollar/mark," said MaryBeth Slack, senior analyst at Thomson Global Markets. \par \par \uc2  A Wall Street-based options broker said customers' requests were very strike-specific, indicating a desire to reduce exposure over the weekend. "It's a very nervous, volatile market right now," the broker said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen implied volatility ended near 16.3/16.6 percent across the curve, with the one-month rising from 15.8/16.0 percent at the open. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/mark volatility ended about 0.3 point higher at a 8.4 percent midpoint. Both the one-week and one-year were quoted late at 8.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  Fresh problems in Russia initially hoisted the dollar above 1.8025 marks before the stock market's plunge slapped it back to 1.7930. The dollar finished the session at 1.7970 marks, while the Dow recovered to end 77.76 lower at 8533.65. \par \par \uc2  Russian Prime Minister Sergei Kiriyenko today told the State Duma lower house of parliament that Russia's problems had only now entered the crisis stage. Parliament called overwhelmingly for President Boris Yeltsin's resignation, blasting his handling of the country's financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  Yeltsin, who has been on summer vacation, was due back in his office Monday for the first time in weeks. \par \par \uc2  Adding to the emerging market malaise, the Venezuelan \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would allow the bolivar to trade more freely within its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bands as the market remained braced for a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar climbed to a high of 145.35 yen from below 143 overnight as rumours the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was preparing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen proved unfounded, options traders said. Spot dollar/yen finished near 144.55. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears are keeping one-month risk reversals well bid for yen calls at 0.6/0.8, though traders said strong interest in yen puts continued from six months to one year. \par \par \uc2  ((Susan Kelly, Chicago Derivatives Desk 312-408-8750 chicago.derivatives.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8l01bk9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
