\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Major  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  fight for limelight. \b0\par\par\uc2 603 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 1998\par 18:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 21 (Reuters) - The dollar firmed against mark and yen on Friday but was unlikely to make further headway next week, as there were too many factors pulling major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in opposing directions, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Each of the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has its own ball and chain," said Rob Miniken, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  "It's Russia for the mark, Asia for the yen and Latin America for the dollar." \par \par \uc2  Trading was light on Friday, dealers said, as the markets tried to absorb falling equity markets, plunging Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Russian crisis and Japanese bank restructuring. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark remained resolute in the face of the storms, rising within a narrow range. \par \par \uc2  "The market's not moving because dollar and mark are both safe havens," said David Brickman, international economist at PaineWebber. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark was trading near session highs at 1.8015/20 at 1458 GMT, from 1.7970/80 late in Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar picked up in Friday's late trade as rating agency Moody's cut Russia's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt ceiling and the long-term deposit ratings of 11 Russian banks. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar's progress was hampered by falls in Wall Street and in Latin American markets, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones index dropped 200 points on global economic worries, and Caracas stocks dropped 5.6 percent in the first half hour of trade, amid fears of a devaluation of the Venezuelan bolivar. \par \par \uc2  The Mexican peso fell, the Colombian peso fell to all-time lows, and Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in general became a focal point for London dealers. \par \par \uc2  "We are looking at everything which is not normally liquid today," said a trader from a U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also made late gains against the yen, in a nervous market even more prone to rumour-led trade than on a normal Friday afternoon, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at session highs at 145.26/49 GMT, from 142.90/00 in late European trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan (LTCB) said it would apply for public funds to strengthen its capital base, forgive a total of 520 billion yen worth of outstanding loans, and dispose of 750 billion yen worth of problem loans. \par \par \uc2  LTCB's proposals needed to become a benchmark for other problem banks in order to restore confidence, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Without stronger government support for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's banking system, any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the yen would fail, analysts added. \par \par \uc2  "If we were to see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now, there would be six weeks and then the market would be back to where it was before or even higher," Brickman said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar last week rose to levels higher than those it reached before the U.S. Federal Reserve and Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen on June 17. \par \par \uc2  But markets are wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after verbal support for the yen from several Japanese officials this week. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was the main beneficiary of safe haven buying on Friday, offering higher interest rates to investors than the alternative safe haven, the Swiss franc, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was buying interest from hedge funds and corporates, as the pound rose to three-week highs against the mark and two-week highs against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The pound was trading at 2.9431/36 marks and $1.6335/45 at 1543 GMT, from 2.9275 marks and $1.6275/85 in late Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  ((Carolyn Cohn, London newsroom +44 171 542 6320, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8l019s3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
