\par\par \b \uc2  Tsy/Sakakibara-Geithner -2: Amid Jittery  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Market \b0\par\par\uc2 224 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 1998\par 21:22\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Geithner is expected to become the No. 2 international\par officer at Treasury next month, when Undersecretary for\par International Affairs David Lipton leaves.\par    The Geithner-Sakakibara meeting comes ahead of a\par meeting between Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin and\par Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa in San Francisco\par in early September. At their tete-a-tete, Geithner and\par Sakakibara are expected to lay the groundwork for the\par meeting between their ministers, which could include\par discussions of Japan's ailing banking industry and the\par country's efforts to pull its economy out of recession.\par    But any talks between Geithner and Sakakibara also come\par at a very sensitive time in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, with\par investors' talk of potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par support the yen ebbing and flowing frequently.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    In this volatile environment, the dollar managed to\par gain nearly three yen on Friday alone, hitting Y145.35 in\par late trading, after Fitch IBCA downgraded six Japanese\par banks and market participants began to feel the Bank of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would not indeed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the yen.\par    In early trading in Asia Friday, the dollar had been\par around Y142.71, compared with Y143.14 late Thursday in New\par York.\par -By Kristi Bahrenburg, tel: (202) 862-9243; or e-mail:\par kristi.bahrenburg@cor.dowjones.com\par \f28 \par