\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar ends US higher, Russia, short-covering prop. \b0\par\par\uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1998\par 23:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 20 (Reuters) - A short-covering rally following overnight Japanese jawboning and hedge fund sales propped dollar/yen sharply higher at the close, while ongoing Russia worries lifted dollar/mark, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had slipped by two-and-a-half yen in Tokyo, its slide greased as senior Finance Ministry official Haruhiko Kuroda said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the appropriate time. \par \par \uc2  Major hedge funds were largely responsible for the early dollar/yen slide, and also the subsequent rebound as they bought back dollar positions, said Chris Melendez, senior dealer at Sanwa Bank California. \par \par \uc2  "Funds are taking advantage of the market ... they know the market is nervous about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so they go in and sell the dollar until they run into (stronger) bids, when they then buy it back," Melendez said. \par \par \uc2  Eisuke Sakakibara, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, reportedly added to his litany of comments warning of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during a visit to the U.S. in Aspen, Colo. \par \par \uc2  At the close, the dollar was higher at 143.05/15 yen, up from its overnight and three-week low of 141.45, and 141.55/65 at the open. Dollar/mark was up at 1.7990/00 from 1.7935/45. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers speculated that Japan's verbal efforts to drive the yen higher might be a move to cushion a negative impact on the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from a potential announcement next week, perhaps surrounding bank failures. \par \par \uc2  "If the dollar is trading at 147-148 yen (during such an announcement), it would break 150 and open up the target of 160, which I don't think they want," said Hillel Waxman, first vice president at Bank Leumi Trust Co of New York. \par \par \uc2  "They're positioning the market to prevent a more significant run that might take place on dollar/yen if we do get negative news," he added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar firmed against the mark with help from cross trading and following news that suggested Russia's financial crisis may deepen in connection to Moscow's recent rouble devaluation and debt moratorium. \par \par \uc2  Sergei Aleksashenko, Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  first deputy chairman, said that some of Russia's biggest banks could be dragged down by the crisis as soon as a few days, which some analysts said puts German lenders at risk. \par \par \uc2  "Russia continues to overhang the German picture despite the comments that Germany is fairly well-positioned not to have much damage from Russia fallout," Waxman said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. military strikes at terrorist bases in Afghanistan and Sudan that President Bill Clinton said played a key role in the bombings of two U.S. embassies in Africa this month fuelled light safe-haven dollar buying, some traders said. \par \par \uc2  Despite firming versus the mark, the dollar endured steady downside pressure from shaky global stock markets and the dollar-negative impact of the White House sex scandal. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/mark is trading sideways, mostly, the equity markets are not looking too healthy and then you've got the Clinton thing in the background," said Robert Fullem, a trader at NatWest Markets. \par \par \uc2  The Dow industrials ended down almost one percent, Russia's RTS index fell more than two percent. Latin American markets also skidded again amid concern that Venezuela is planning to devalue its bolivar despite denials and aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Caracas. \par \par \uc2  In other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, the dollar was higher at 1.5065/75 Swiss francs from 1.5042/52 at the open and rose to Canadian $1.5312/17 from C$1.5245/50. The British pound was unchanged at $1.6295/05 and the Australian dollar fell to $0.5869/74 from $0.5917/22. \par \par \uc2  ((-Al Yoon, N.A. Treasury Desk, 212-859-1672)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8k016zg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
